WEBVTT

﻿1
00:00:26.417 --> 00:00:29.500
I was in my office at work

2
00:00:29.584 --> 00:00:32.834
and I got a call
from an international number.

3
00:00:32.918 --> 00:00:34.709
Through the last
three years.

4
00:00:34.792 --> 00:00:37.584
when it was an international
call. I'd answer it.

5
00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:39.542
It was a reporter in Dublin

6
00:00:41.667 --> 00:00:44.292
who wanted a reaction
to the story.

7
00:00:44.375 --> 00:00:47.250
I said.
"Reaction for what?"

8
00:00:47.334 --> 00:00:50.792
They were really caught back
by that question. obviously.

9
00:00:50.876 --> 00:00:55.417
And they said. "I'll call
you back in five minutes."

10
00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:00.959
And so then I went online
and saw the picture.

11
00:01:06.334 --> 00:01:09.125
It's not the way
you want to find out.

12
00:01:09.209 --> 00:01:10.792
I never. ever imagined

13
00:01:10.876 --> 00:01:15.500
that it would end
in that fashion. right?

14
00:01:18.334 --> 00:01:21.459
For days that first week.

15
00:01:21.542 --> 00:01:24.542
I just would constantly
see that image and...

16
00:01:26.876 --> 00:01:29.459
It's been tough.

17
00:01:29.542 --> 00:01:32.667
You go through
those emotional swings.

18
00:01:32.751 --> 00:01:37.083
"Why the hell did you go
back that second time?"

19
00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:39.042
But I keep coming back
to the same answer.

20
00:02:08.626 --> 00:02:10.417
Name the sports newspaper

21
00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.959
that hit US newsstands
in 1990.

22
00:02:13.042 --> 00:02:14.209
James?

23
00:02:14.292 --> 00:02:16.250
- "The National."
- It was.

24
00:02:58.500 --> 00:02:59.918
Ladies and gentlemen.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.520
please help me welcome
Mr. James Foley.

26
00:03:08.167 --> 00:03:10.375
Thanks for that generous...

27
00:03:10.459 --> 00:03:12.876
Overly generous...

28
00:03:12.959 --> 00:03:14.584
...introduction.

29
00:03:14.667 --> 00:03:17.417
I'm definitely
not a hero

30
00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:18.959
or noble or anything.

31
00:03:19.042 --> 00:03:22.542
I'm just trying
to do my work

32
00:03:22.626 --> 00:03:25.459
and got into
a little bit of trouble.

33
00:03:30.334 --> 00:03:33.918
I arrived
in Benghazi in mid-March.

34
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:35.209
and the night I arrived.

35
00:03:35.292 --> 00:03:37.584
I heard nothing
but bombs and gunfire.

36
00:03:39.542 --> 00:03:41.292
I wondered.
"What's going on?"

37
00:03:41.375 --> 00:03:44.292
Another journalist said.
"The bombs. that's gelatina.

38
00:03:44.375 --> 00:03:46.542
That's how they fish."

39
00:03:48.209 --> 00:03:49.709
Blow the fish up.

40
00:03:49.792 --> 00:03:51.209
"And the gunfire?

41
00:03:51.292 --> 00:03:52.918
No. that's
celebratory gunfire."

42
00:03:56.292 --> 00:03:58.000
If Gaddafi
comes to Benghazi.

43
00:03:58.083 --> 00:04:00.500
are you guys
prepared to fight?

44
00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:16.876
I think in some ways

45
00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:19.584
Libya was
a turning point for Jim.

46
00:04:19.667 --> 00:04:21.375
I was starting to see

47
00:04:21.459 --> 00:04:23.292
his trying to figure out

48
00:04:23.375 --> 00:04:25.459
where he belonged
in the world.

49
00:04:25.542 --> 00:04:27.751
He tried
the Teach for America.

50
00:04:27.834 --> 00:04:32.083
and then Chicago he worked
at the Cook County Jail.

51
00:04:32.167 --> 00:04:34.292
He anguished
over his teaching.

52
00:04:34.375 --> 00:04:36.667
He didn't think he was
a good enough teacher.

53
00:04:36.751 --> 00:04:40.334
He wanted to write
and he loved people

54
00:04:40.417 --> 00:04:41.667
and liked to interact.

55
00:04:41.751 --> 00:04:43.042
tell people's stories.

56
00:04:43.125 --> 00:04:45.042
So when Jim decided
he wanted to go

57
00:04:45.125 --> 00:04:47.584
into journalism
from teaching.

58
00:04:47.667 --> 00:04:50.000
at first we thought
that would be good.

59
00:04:50.083 --> 00:04:52.125
"Maybe that'll be
a better fit. Jim."

60
00:04:52.209 --> 00:04:56.709
But when he decided
to do conflict journalism.

61
00:04:56.792 --> 00:04:58.542
that was a whole
different deal.

62
00:04:58.626 --> 00:05:01.209
Well. he didn't
exactly tell us.

63
00:05:02.375 --> 00:05:05.667
He graduated from Medill

64
00:05:05.751 --> 00:05:08.125
and we said. "Jim. what
are you going to do?"

65
00:05:08.209 --> 00:05:10.042
And he said.
"Well. I'm working on it."

66
00:05:10.125 --> 00:05:13.876
The next thing we knew.
he was going to go to Iraq

67
00:05:13.959 --> 00:05:16.167
embedded with
the Indiana National Guard.

68
00:05:19.459 --> 00:05:23.125
"Well. I guess I'm a conflict
journalist at this point."

69
00:05:23.209 --> 00:05:25.667
So that's how we found out.

70
00:05:28.584 --> 00:05:30.250
It's an all-out sprint.

71
00:05:30.334 --> 00:05:32.876
an exhilarating and dangerous
run for cover.

72
00:05:32.959 --> 00:05:34.626
You got him?

73
00:05:34.709 --> 00:05:37.375
When Jim started
the journalism path.

74
00:05:37.459 --> 00:05:42.918
we did have some heated
discussions on views

75
00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:44.834
and the military
and whatnot.

76
00:05:44.918 --> 00:05:48.751
My views are a little bit
more conservative than Jim's.

77
00:05:48.834 --> 00:05:52.083
I would say
a little bit more liberal.

78
00:05:52.167 --> 00:05:53.918
But when he did his embed.

79
00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:58.209
I think. is where
we came closer together.

80
00:05:58.292 --> 00:05:59.751
As soon as the shooting
died down.

81
00:05:59.834 --> 00:06:02.834
the platoon discovered
a body limp on the roof.

82
00:06:05.626 --> 00:06:07.667
Do they know
who the guy is?

83
00:06:07.751 --> 00:06:09.792
Yes.

84
00:06:09.876 --> 00:06:11.083
His family members.

85
00:06:11.167 --> 00:06:12.792
- These are the family?
- Yeah.

86
00:06:22.709 --> 00:06:25.918
John. Jr.: Then he came back
to my house and he told me...

87
00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:30.292
He was like. "John. I'm
thinking about going to Libya."

88
00:06:33.542 --> 00:06:36.375
I'm like. "Jim.
that's a horrible idea.

89
00:06:36.459 --> 00:06:40.667
That is absolutely
a horrible idea.

90
00:06:40.751 --> 00:06:45.959
If you go over there.
no one's coming for you.

91
00:06:46.042 --> 00:06:48.709
Why would you put
your life in danger?

92
00:06:48.792 --> 00:06:50.751
We're dropping bombs
over there.

93
00:06:50.834 --> 00:06:53.417
And God forbid
if you go over there

94
00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:56.918
and we accidentally
kill you."

95
00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:00.959
I mean. why?

96
00:07:01.042 --> 00:07:03.167
Why?

97
00:07:06.584 --> 00:07:08.375
Libya was very exciting
as a journalist

98
00:07:08.459 --> 00:07:10.792
because you had this chance
to talk directly to people.

99
00:07:10.876 --> 00:07:12.156
to see exactly
what was going on.

100
00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:16.000
There were no
US soldiers anymore.

101
00:07:16.083 --> 00:07:17.834
No organized army
anymore telling you

102
00:07:17.918 --> 00:07:19.198
what you should
and shouldn't do.

103
00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:22.959
But it was also
extremely dangerous.

104
00:07:25.709 --> 00:07:27.667
I was actually talking
to one of my buddies

105
00:07:27.751 --> 00:07:29.459
boasting
about my brother Jim

106
00:07:29.542 --> 00:07:31.542
and how he's
this journalist

107
00:07:31.626 --> 00:07:34.959
and is super crazy.
but badass at the same time.

108
00:07:35.042 --> 00:07:37.626
I was like. "Yeah.
my brother's a badass.

109
00:07:37.709 --> 00:07:39.500
He's crazy.
He's awesome."

110
00:07:39.584 --> 00:07:41.250
I also was
very naive myself.

111
00:07:41.334 --> 00:07:42.935
I didn't know exactly
what that would mean.

112
00:07:42.959 --> 00:07:44.292
I didn't know
he was actually

113
00:07:44.375 --> 00:07:46.417
going to be immersed
in actual crossfire.

114
00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:48.334
There's snipers
in this building

115
00:07:48.417 --> 00:07:50.709
about one kilometer away.

116
00:07:50.792 --> 00:07:52.209
The group decided to go in.

117
00:07:52.292 --> 00:07:54.959
Heavy fighting.

118
00:07:55.042 --> 00:07:57.209
Heavy casualties.
About 30 wounded.

119
00:07:57.292 --> 00:08:00.167
two dead
as of this morning.

120
00:08:01.751 --> 00:08:03.042
There's one video

121
00:08:03.125 --> 00:08:04.751
that always
stands out to me.

122
00:08:04.834 --> 00:08:08.209
It's when he's standing
in the square.

123
00:08:08.292 --> 00:08:11.000
There does seem to be
a strong sense

124
00:08:11.083 --> 00:08:12.667
that they won't
give up the fight.

125
00:08:12.751 --> 00:08:15.626
And that they are a force
of young fighting men.

126
00:08:15.709 --> 00:08:17.167
Although unorganized.

127
00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:21.209
there's plenty of will
to hold out here.

128
00:08:21.292 --> 00:08:22.834
How does my guy
know this stuff?

129
00:08:22.918 --> 00:08:24.459
How does Jim Foley...

130
00:08:24.542 --> 00:08:27.459
He's just my meathead
friend. you know?

131
00:08:27.542 --> 00:08:32.125
It was so foreign to me
in terms of an experience.

132
00:08:32.209 --> 00:08:34.167
I'm like.
"How do you get into Libya?

133
00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:36.792
You're a freelancer?
Who wants this?

134
00:08:36.876 --> 00:08:38.709
Are you getting paid?"

135
00:08:38.792 --> 00:08:41.083
This is Jim Foley reporting
from downtown Benghazi.

136
00:08:41.167 --> 00:08:43.959
Revolutionary Square.
"Global Post."

137
00:08:45.834 --> 00:08:49.042
Jim was there
at the early stage

138
00:08:49.125 --> 00:08:50.542
of this movement
of there being

139
00:08:50.626 --> 00:08:53.751
more freelancers
in conflict areas.

140
00:08:55.375 --> 00:08:58.375
The world
has changed so much

141
00:08:58.459 --> 00:08:59.876
in terms of
digital publishing

142
00:08:59.959 --> 00:09:03.959
and newspapers started
to eliminate things

143
00:09:04.042 --> 00:09:06.667
that they didn't see
as essential.

144
00:09:06.751 --> 00:09:10.125
International coverage
dwindled down to very little.

145
00:09:10.209 --> 00:09:13.626
so we saw an opportunity
to fill that void

146
00:09:13.709 --> 00:09:17.125
and we needed to work
with freelancers.

147
00:09:17.209 --> 00:09:19.292
Freelancers decide
to work together

148
00:09:19.375 --> 00:09:24.417
just on the basis of this
initial quick-read chemistry.

149
00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:28.751
I saw this new guy
who I hadn't met before.

150
00:09:28.834 --> 00:09:31.125
He looked friendly enough.
so I said. "Hey. what's up?"

151
00:09:31.209 --> 00:09:34.751
He said. "Not much.
Going to the frontline."

152
00:09:34.834 --> 00:09:37.500
He's heard
a lot about Libya

153
00:09:37.584 --> 00:09:40.667
and the fact that
it was very cheap to work.

154
00:09:40.751 --> 00:09:44.834
Rebels and protestors were eager
to show us their side of the story.

155
00:09:44.918 --> 00:09:47.334
They were driving us
all over for free.

156
00:09:47.417 --> 00:09:50.959
They were translating
for us for free.

157
00:09:51.042 --> 00:09:54.209
Many of us never really
experienced the luxury

158
00:09:54.292 --> 00:09:56.667
of journalism
in its heyday.

159
00:09:56.751 --> 00:10:00.083
What we do is journalism
on a shoestring budget.

160
00:10:00.167 --> 00:10:04.209
So we've had to be a lot
more resourceful in a way

161
00:10:04.292 --> 00:10:06.667
and just more street savvy.

162
00:10:06.751 --> 00:10:10.375
I think. in a sense. the way
we all got to know each other

163
00:10:10.459 --> 00:10:13.209
was the Africa Hotel
in Benghazi.

164
00:10:13.292 --> 00:10:16.334
It was the cheapest hotel.
and the crappiest.

165
00:10:16.417 --> 00:10:17.959
and we were all
staying there.

166
00:10:25.417 --> 00:10:28.542
I'd seen Jim talking
to a few other journalists.

167
00:10:28.626 --> 00:10:31.876
and he was just really
friendly with everybody.

168
00:10:31.959 --> 00:10:33.751
It was unusual
in a place like that.

169
00:10:33.834 --> 00:10:36.626
There's still an edge of
competitiveness in that environment.

170
00:10:36.709 --> 00:10:39.626
whereas Jim was just like.
"Yeah. whatever."

171
00:10:39.709 --> 00:10:41.918
He gave off a really good
first impression.

172
00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:45.000
and it helps that he's a
super good-looking guy.

173
00:10:45.083 --> 00:10:48.292
I was just like. "Who is
this guy? Who are you?"

174
00:10:51.417 --> 00:10:55.792
There wasn't anything mundane
about the man whatsoever.

175
00:10:55.876 --> 00:10:58.667
He never really projected
himself onto a situation

176
00:10:58.751 --> 00:11:02.667
and he dealt
well with people.

177
00:11:02.751 --> 00:11:04.834
Who are the Libyan rebels

178
00:11:04.918 --> 00:11:07.292
determined to overthrow
42 years of dictatorship?

179
00:11:13.334 --> 00:11:14.751
- Offshore welder.
- Platform. yes.

180
00:11:14.834 --> 00:11:17.292
Okay. Dangerous job.

181
00:11:18.876 --> 00:11:20.584
They're brave
as individuals.

182
00:11:20.667 --> 00:11:23.584
but many show a dangerous
lack of weapons training.

183
00:11:25.584 --> 00:11:27.125
There were so many
freelancers

184
00:11:27.209 --> 00:11:29.042
who came in
at that point in time

185
00:11:29.125 --> 00:11:31.959
who were so new.
Jim and I among them.

186
00:11:32.042 --> 00:11:35.417
and I found out only later
that there were a number

187
00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:38.167
of much more experienced
photojournalists

188
00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:40.834
who had made one or
two trips to the frontline

189
00:11:40.918 --> 00:11:43.751
and said. "Fuck it.
This is way too dangerous."

190
00:12:26.918 --> 00:12:28.834
Jim had a high tolerance
for danger.

191
00:12:28.918 --> 00:12:31.792
Shit.

192
00:12:31.876 --> 00:12:35.417
Sure. he was drawn to that.
All of us are. in a way.

193
00:12:35.500 --> 00:12:37.083
He was cool as a cucumber
as well.

194
00:12:37.167 --> 00:12:41.334
wearing this little
tiny vest he used to wear.

195
00:12:41.417 --> 00:12:43.792
The fact that
he stayed so calm

196
00:12:43.876 --> 00:12:48.000
made it easy to feel calm
in that situation.

197
00:12:48.083 --> 00:12:50.500
But. of course.
sometimes I was just like.

198
00:12:50.584 --> 00:12:52.792
"That's crazy.
I'm not going there with you."

199
00:12:56.042 --> 00:12:57.751
I would come back
to Benghazi

200
00:12:57.834 --> 00:12:59.792
and there was stuff
going on there.

201
00:12:59.876 --> 00:13:01.542
There was families

202
00:13:01.626 --> 00:13:04.167
and they were out there
in the protests.

203
00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:07.125
and they were out there
maybe handling medical supplies.

204
00:13:07.209 --> 00:13:09.292
which is probably
more important

205
00:13:09.375 --> 00:13:11.292
to what this
revolutionary movement was.

206
00:13:11.375 --> 00:13:14.834
But then being called out
to that frontline again

207
00:13:14.918 --> 00:13:18.751
like some kind of
siren song...

208
00:13:22.250 --> 00:13:24.542
It was one of those mornings
where we decided

209
00:13:24.626 --> 00:13:26.500
we were gonna
get out there early.

210
00:13:26.584 --> 00:13:28.185
We wanted to get a
fresh look at the frontlines.

211
00:13:28.209 --> 00:13:30.375
It was myself.
Clare Gillis.

212
00:13:30.459 --> 00:13:32.751
Manu Brabo.
and Anton Hammerl.

213
00:13:32.834 --> 00:13:34.667
a South African
photojournalist.

214
00:13:34.751 --> 00:13:36.709
- Meet my friend!
- Anton.

215
00:13:36.792 --> 00:13:38.375
Anton!

216
00:13:38.459 --> 00:13:40.542
And what it was. really.
was just a highway.

217
00:13:40.626 --> 00:13:42.751
a coastal highway
going all across Libya.

218
00:13:42.834 --> 00:13:45.125
And this is where
the battle was going on.

219
00:13:45.209 --> 00:13:48.667
So it was kind of like
a Mad Max type war.

220
00:13:50.626 --> 00:13:53.083
This was something common
that some reporters did.

221
00:13:53.167 --> 00:13:55.207
Freelancers like myself
who didn't have big budgets.

222
00:13:55.250 --> 00:13:57.500
we'd jump in
with the rebels.

223
00:13:57.584 --> 00:14:00.959
It was at your own risk if you
wanted to go further or not.

224
00:14:03.209 --> 00:14:06.417
We got to the point where
we saw another group of rebels

225
00:14:06.500 --> 00:14:09.751
saying Gaddafi forces
are 300 meters away.

226
00:14:09.834 --> 00:14:11.083
And myself.
looking at Clare.

227
00:14:11.167 --> 00:14:13.167
like.
"That's impossible."

228
00:14:13.250 --> 00:14:16.250
I remember Anton
turning to me

229
00:14:16.334 --> 00:14:18.918
and saying.
"Hey. this isn't safe."

230
00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:22.375
But we didn't turn around.

231
00:14:22.459 --> 00:14:25.834
We said. "Let's get off
the road anyways."

232
00:14:25.918 --> 00:14:27.918
That was the exact
wrong thing to do.

233
00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:32.500
Two heavily armed
Gaddafi pickup trucks

234
00:14:32.584 --> 00:14:35.125
came over
that rise firing.

235
00:14:37.375 --> 00:14:38.667
I remember so clearly

236
00:14:38.751 --> 00:14:41.459
the sound of it.
the volume of it.

237
00:14:41.542 --> 00:14:45.459
the sound of something
heating metal.

238
00:14:45.542 --> 00:14:48.000
And I remember
hoping against hope

239
00:14:48.083 --> 00:14:50.918
that there would be
some kind of out out of this.

240
00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:54.375
there was some kind
of trapdoor in time.

241
00:14:58.334 --> 00:15:01.083
I crawled back
to the sand dune.

242
00:15:01.167 --> 00:15:03.375
Anton was at the other
sand dune in front of me.

243
00:15:03.459 --> 00:15:05.250
I heard him call for help.

244
00:15:07.083 --> 00:15:10.083
It appeared he was cut across
the midsection with AK fire.

245
00:15:10.167 --> 00:15:12.125
and there was
a serious amount of blood.

246
00:15:12.209 --> 00:15:13.584
He had already
lost consciousness

247
00:15:13.667 --> 00:15:15.209
and probably already died.

248
00:15:20.083 --> 00:15:22.626
A group of young soldiers
approached me

249
00:15:22.709 --> 00:15:25.542
and we were thrown
in the back of a truck.

250
00:15:25.626 --> 00:15:29.459
I remember getting
photographed with a cell phone

251
00:15:29.542 --> 00:15:33.500
and thinking this is where
they find all these photographs

252
00:15:33.584 --> 00:15:36.584
that are evidence
of war crimes someday.

253
00:15:36.667 --> 00:15:39.083
and realizing
this is me now.

254
00:15:47.626 --> 00:15:49.250
I was with my mother.

255
00:15:49.334 --> 00:15:51.876
We were out to lunch
and I received a phone call.

256
00:15:51.959 --> 00:15:56.918
And that's how
we first heard. you know?

257
00:15:57.000 --> 00:15:59.792
I think I was in denial
about how dangerous

258
00:15:59.876 --> 00:16:03.876
this really was. Brian.

259
00:16:03.959 --> 00:16:07.083
I was furious.

260
00:16:07.167 --> 00:16:09.083
just furious.

261
00:16:09.167 --> 00:16:11.375
Scared for him.
furious.

262
00:16:11.459 --> 00:16:13.709
I hate to revisit
it. but it was like.

263
00:16:13.792 --> 00:16:16.542
"I told you. Jim."

264
00:16:16.626 --> 00:16:20.709
I think we all went through
the stages of total shock

265
00:16:20.792 --> 00:16:25.125
and then just.
"What are we going to do?"

266
00:16:25.209 --> 00:16:27.000
And then anger.

267
00:16:27.083 --> 00:16:29.500
After all.
we're family. you know?

268
00:16:32.918 --> 00:16:34.667
You're so humbled.

269
00:16:34.751 --> 00:16:36.542
You lost everything...

270
00:16:36.626 --> 00:16:39.334
Your freedom.
your control.

271
00:16:39.417 --> 00:16:41.167
your ability
to talk to anybody

272
00:16:41.250 --> 00:16:42.584
and tell anybody
you're okay.

273
00:16:42.667 --> 00:16:44.417
Thinking one minute.
"Oh. yeah.

274
00:16:44.500 --> 00:16:46.334
I'm a foreign
correspondent."

275
00:16:46.417 --> 00:16:50.083
The next minute. somebody
who you respect killed

276
00:16:50.167 --> 00:16:52.709
and you have nothing.

277
00:16:56.709 --> 00:16:59.250
Jim was concerned

278
00:16:59.334 --> 00:17:02.167
that his own
competitiveness

279
00:17:02.250 --> 00:17:04.834
with Manu. with Anton.
with himself.

280
00:17:04.918 --> 00:17:08.375
his own sort of
macho aggressiveness

281
00:17:08.459 --> 00:17:10.792
had driven him
to make decisions

282
00:17:10.876 --> 00:17:12.417
that were not
the best decisions.

283
00:17:12.500 --> 00:17:15.167
We were all questioning
our judgment.

284
00:17:15.250 --> 00:17:18.542
One of the main things
that affected all of us

285
00:17:18.626 --> 00:17:24.459
is that Anton had kids
and none of us do.

286
00:17:24.542 --> 00:17:26.918
Every day I have to
deal with the fact

287
00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:31.292
that Anton is not going to
ever see his three kids anymore.

288
00:17:31.375 --> 00:17:33.959
and I was part of that
decision-making process...

289
00:17:36.626 --> 00:17:38.000
that took him away...

290
00:17:38.083 --> 00:17:40.542
That took him away
from his kids and his wife.

291
00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:59.042
I had a lot of time
to play over those moments.

292
00:17:59.125 --> 00:18:02.125
especially that one day
when we were captured.

293
00:18:02.209 --> 00:18:04.667
I tried to question myself.
"What are you reporting on?

294
00:18:04.751 --> 00:18:07.000
What is this all about?"

295
00:18:07.083 --> 00:18:08.667
It was quickly apparent

296
00:18:08.751 --> 00:18:11.334
that this was about
being what you think

297
00:18:11.417 --> 00:18:13.626
is an authentic
conflict correspondent.

298
00:18:13.709 --> 00:18:16.125
seeing the frontline.
and it not being enough

299
00:18:16.209 --> 00:18:18.334
to just see it
from a distance.

300
00:18:18.417 --> 00:18:20.667
but to push it
to the next level.

301
00:18:20.751 --> 00:18:23.000
You were basically
waiting to get shelled.

302
00:18:23.083 --> 00:18:26.334
and the question is.
"Why?"

303
00:18:26.417 --> 00:18:28.334
You know.
"Why are you doing this?"

304
00:18:32.292 --> 00:18:34.417
A lot of us were just
scratching our head. right?

305
00:18:34.500 --> 00:18:37.125
There's no money.
Maybe you get a story

306
00:18:37.209 --> 00:18:38.792
here and there
that you sell.

307
00:18:38.876 --> 00:18:40.918
I used to loan him money.

308
00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:44.292
My credit report
has one ding on it.

309
00:18:44.375 --> 00:18:47.792
and it's the one loan
that I cosigned with Jim.

310
00:18:47.876 --> 00:18:53.250
I think Jimmy was just
a little outside the lines.

311
00:18:53.334 --> 00:18:55.292
He was wicked disorganized.

312
00:18:55.375 --> 00:18:57.083
We had to tell him
to come to dinner.

313
00:18:57.167 --> 00:19:00.209
If dinner was at 5:00.
we'd tell him it was at 3:00.

314
00:19:00.292 --> 00:19:02.042
So he'd show up at 5:00.

315
00:19:02.125 --> 00:19:03.959
He always lives
in the moment.

316
00:19:04.042 --> 00:19:05.542
I don't know
if Mike ever told you

317
00:19:05.626 --> 00:19:08.000
that Jim was late
to his wedding.

318
00:19:08.083 --> 00:19:10.167
"John. can I sleep
at your house?

319
00:19:10.250 --> 00:19:12.792
John. can my friend
stay at your house?"

320
00:19:12.876 --> 00:19:16.250
"Yes. Jim. yes."

321
00:19:16.334 --> 00:19:19.500
Even though you were like.
"Jim. just wake up!

322
00:19:19.584 --> 00:19:21.542
Wake up!
What are you doing?

323
00:19:21.626 --> 00:19:22.834
Get a real job.

324
00:19:22.918 --> 00:19:25.876
Start saving up
for retirement."

325
00:19:25.959 --> 00:19:28.626
I remember I said. "Why don't
you come to Chicago?

326
00:19:28.709 --> 00:19:30.584
My dad runs this boot camp

327
00:19:30.667 --> 00:19:32.834
that's teaching
young felons."

328
00:19:32.918 --> 00:19:34.643
He goes there for the interview
and I call him afterwards

329
00:19:34.667 --> 00:19:36.459
and I'm like.
"How'd the interview go?"

330
00:19:36.542 --> 00:19:39.584
He's like. "It was great.
I think it went great."

331
00:19:39.667 --> 00:19:41.375
I call my dad and he goes.

332
00:19:41.459 --> 00:19:42.959
"He said it went great.
did he?

333
00:19:43.042 --> 00:19:45.334
Because I just talked to
the lady at the hiring board.

334
00:19:45.417 --> 00:19:48.209
and when she went out there.
she found Jim sleeping

335
00:19:48.292 --> 00:19:50.209
with his head
back on the wall

336
00:19:50.292 --> 00:19:51.810
and he's wearing jeans with
paint or something all over them."

337
00:19:51.834 --> 00:19:53.584
I call Jim back.
I'm like. "Jim.

338
00:19:53.667 --> 00:19:55.542
what the hell happened
at the interview?"

339
00:19:55.626 --> 00:19:57.292
He's like.
"It was hot in there. Tom.

340
00:19:57.375 --> 00:19:59.709
It was hot. I just put my
head back and I nodded off."

341
00:19:59.792 --> 00:20:01.792
I'm like. "Did you have
jeans on with paint?"

342
00:20:01.876 --> 00:20:04.042
And he's like. "Yeah. they
had these symbols on them.

343
00:20:04.125 --> 00:20:06.435
What was I supposed to wear?"
I'm like. "Jim. it's an interview.

344
00:20:06.459 --> 00:20:08.375
It's an interview.
You can't do this."

345
00:20:08.459 --> 00:20:11.417
So my dad always says that
Jim had a million-dollar resume

346
00:20:11.500 --> 00:20:14.125
and a 10-cent interview.

347
00:20:14.209 --> 00:20:16.918
He came and he started
teaching reading and writing.

348
00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:20.292
some literature. and he was
really good with these kids.

349
00:20:20.375 --> 00:20:22.459
because Jim was into
hip-hop and rap

350
00:20:22.542 --> 00:20:24.626
and he liked
to grab a mic himself.

351
00:20:24.709 --> 00:20:26.375
Jim connected
with these guys.

352
00:20:26.459 --> 00:20:28.626
He'd find out about their
backgrounds. talk to them.

353
00:20:28.709 --> 00:20:30.334
I think that
really hit him.

354
00:20:30.417 --> 00:20:31.918
He was seeing young guys

355
00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:33.959
that came from
terrible neighborhoods

356
00:20:34.042 --> 00:20:35.584
that never really
had a chance.

357
00:20:35.667 --> 00:20:37.209
I think he started seeing

358
00:20:37.292 --> 00:20:39.417
there are people who need
their stories told.

359
00:20:43.709 --> 00:20:46.584
The prison that we were
transferred to

360
00:20:46.667 --> 00:20:50.375
was filled with all these political
prisoners from all walks of life.

361
00:20:50.459 --> 00:20:52.584
Some were fishermen.
some were engineers.

362
00:20:52.667 --> 00:20:54.959
It was the warmth
of these Libyans that said.

363
00:20:55.042 --> 00:20:56.792
"Here you're our guest.
Take this bed.

364
00:20:56.876 --> 00:20:58.560
You don't have a cigarette?
Here's a cigarette."

365
00:20:58.584 --> 00:21:00.264
"You want an extra piece
of chicken? Here."

366
00:21:02.167 --> 00:21:04.250
These guys understand
that I'm a journalist

367
00:21:04.334 --> 00:21:06.000
and I'm trying
to get the truth.

368
00:21:06.083 --> 00:21:07.935
and perhaps wrongly
they think I'm on their side.

369
00:21:07.959 --> 00:21:10.000
but I certainly
feel like it now.

370
00:21:17.500 --> 00:21:19.300
You know that if you
are kind of without hope.

371
00:21:19.375 --> 00:21:20.959
you're totally humbled.

372
00:21:21.042 --> 00:21:24.209
you tend to go to your faith
if you have faith.

373
00:21:24.292 --> 00:21:26.918
Praying five times a day...
It was the life.

374
00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:29.751
It was their only source
of life in prison.

375
00:21:29.834 --> 00:21:32.000
I was up nights
talking to them.

376
00:21:32.083 --> 00:21:34.709
Eventually. one of them said.
"Why don't you pray with us?"

377
00:21:34.792 --> 00:21:36.709
And I said.
"No. I'll pray to Isa.

378
00:21:36.792 --> 00:21:38.393
I pray to Jesus
when you guys are praying."

379
00:21:38.417 --> 00:21:41.626
He sort of looked at me
like. "Okay."

380
00:21:41.709 --> 00:21:43.918
I guess
I'm an experiential person.

381
00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:45.626
so I started
praying with them.

382
00:21:49.667 --> 00:21:53.042
Little did I know. I just
proclaimed my conversion to Islam.

383
00:21:54.918 --> 00:21:58.834
So from then on out. I prayed
with them five times a day.

384
00:21:58.918 --> 00:22:01.959
And it was just...
It was so powerful.

385
00:22:02.042 --> 00:22:04.542
and it was something
that I needed to do.

386
00:22:04.626 --> 00:22:06.042
But it was difficult.

387
00:22:06.125 --> 00:22:08.709
I was thinking.
"Jesus. am I praying to Allah?

388
00:22:08.792 --> 00:22:12.083
Am I violating
my belief in you?"

389
00:22:12.167 --> 00:22:14.167
I don't have
an answer to that.

390
00:22:14.250 --> 00:22:16.459
I just know that I was
authentically praying with them

391
00:22:16.542 --> 00:22:19.125
and I was authentically
also praying to Jesus.

392
00:22:23.459 --> 00:22:26.500
Jim and I were
very close in age.

393
00:22:26.584 --> 00:22:30.209
look enough alike that
I used his ID in college.

394
00:22:30.292 --> 00:22:31.584
but that's about it.

395
00:22:31.667 --> 00:22:33.334
We couldn't be
more different.

396
00:22:33.417 --> 00:22:36.834
Jim was really different
from most of us. right?

397
00:22:36.918 --> 00:22:39.167
I mean. to do
what he went on to do.

398
00:22:39.250 --> 00:22:41.709
it's not a normal path.

399
00:22:41.792 --> 00:22:43.876
When Jim was taken
in Libya.

400
00:22:43.959 --> 00:22:46.667
I just went into
overdrive at that point.

401
00:22:46.751 --> 00:22:49.334
It's a nightmare.
Each day it becomes harder

402
00:22:49.417 --> 00:22:51.209
with the lack
of information.

403
00:22:51.292 --> 00:22:52.918
We know he's in Tripoli.

404
00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:54.667
We believe he's in
a detention center.

405
00:22:54.751 --> 00:22:58.334
We really don't know
much more beyond that.

406
00:22:58.417 --> 00:23:01.375
We organized this huge group
of Jimmy's friends.

407
00:23:01.459 --> 00:23:03.417
We called them FOJs...
Friends of Jim.

408
00:23:03.500 --> 00:23:05.125
We had a lot
of outside help.

409
00:23:05.209 --> 00:23:07.042
but Michael was sort of
the CEO of the group.

410
00:23:07.125 --> 00:23:09.792
His statement was
there are no measurables

411
00:23:09.876 --> 00:23:11.709
other than getting
Jim home.

412
00:23:11.792 --> 00:23:15.626
Our biggest fear is that it
becomes yesterday's story

413
00:23:15.709 --> 00:23:17.500
and people
forget about it.

414
00:23:17.584 --> 00:23:19.667
We love Jim
and we miss him.

415
00:23:19.751 --> 00:23:21.667
We want him home.

416
00:23:23.792 --> 00:23:25.334
Tell me about the release.

417
00:23:25.417 --> 00:23:27.459
What was that day like
for you guys?

418
00:23:29.584 --> 00:23:31.792
After successful
diplomatic negotiations.

419
00:23:31.876 --> 00:23:35.250
two American journalists and a
Spanish journalist are finally going home

420
00:23:35.334 --> 00:23:36.959
after being kidnapped
and detained

421
00:23:37.042 --> 00:23:38.918
by the Libyan government
forces for 44 days.

422
00:23:39.042 --> 00:23:40.834
- What is your name?
- Manu Brabo.

423
00:23:40.918 --> 00:23:43.042
- Were you okay? Were you looked after?
- I'm fine.

424
00:23:43.125 --> 00:23:45.709
If you didn't catch
it the first time around.

425
00:23:45.792 --> 00:23:47.250
get it from your buddy.

426
00:23:47.334 --> 00:23:49.918
Guys. could you please
give them some space?

427
00:23:51.959 --> 00:23:54.018
What was really interesting
about that whole process.

428
00:23:54.042 --> 00:23:56.834
there was a day where we
were making some progress

429
00:23:56.918 --> 00:23:59.167
and I just decided
that I have to go.

430
00:23:59.250 --> 00:24:00.751
I have to get on
a plane and go.

431
00:24:00.834 --> 00:24:03.167
I don't know.
I can't describe it.

432
00:24:03.250 --> 00:24:05.792
But I knew...
At least felt strongly...

433
00:24:05.876 --> 00:24:09.250
That it was going to
work out.

434
00:24:09.334 --> 00:24:11.959
All the security guys
wanted to debrief him first.

435
00:24:12.042 --> 00:24:13.393
and finally I said.
"Enough of this shit"

436
00:24:13.417 --> 00:24:15.584
and just went into
the hotel room.

437
00:24:15.667 --> 00:24:18.167
I remember seeing Jim.
he had a full beard.

438
00:24:18.250 --> 00:24:21.626
and he just grabbed me
and said. "Mike."

439
00:24:21.709 --> 00:24:24.500
We both kind of just
grabbed each other's arms.

440
00:24:24.584 --> 00:24:26.792
like. just to...
Is it real?

441
00:24:26.876 --> 00:24:28.250
I ordered pizza

442
00:24:28.334 --> 00:24:30.059
and as many Heinekens
as they could put in a car

443
00:24:30.083 --> 00:24:31.751
and about two packs
of cigarettes

444
00:24:31.834 --> 00:24:33.518
because I knew that's all
they had over there.

445
00:24:33.542 --> 00:24:38.042
I looked at them and I said.
"I guess I got this one."

446
00:24:38.125 --> 00:24:39.792
and we just stayed up
all night.

447
00:24:43.042 --> 00:24:44.542
All the family
and friends.

448
00:24:44.626 --> 00:24:47.000
we're all together
waiting for them.

449
00:24:49.417 --> 00:24:50.959
And all of a sudden we see

450
00:24:51.042 --> 00:24:54.334
these blue lights
just rushing at us.

451
00:24:55.876 --> 00:24:57.459
We're like.
"Oh. my God. this is Jim.

452
00:24:57.542 --> 00:24:59.834
This is...
He's actually here."

453
00:24:59.918 --> 00:25:01.834
I don't know.
it was like a movie.

454
00:25:01.918 --> 00:25:04.626
He was so happy
to see everybody.

455
00:25:19.834 --> 00:25:21.834
You have a close call.

456
00:25:21.918 --> 00:25:23.878
That's pure luck that
you didn't get killed there.

457
00:25:23.918 --> 00:25:27.667
It's not worth seeing your
mother or father bawling

458
00:25:27.751 --> 00:25:30.334
and worrying about
your grandmother dying

459
00:25:30.417 --> 00:25:31.584
because you're in prison.

460
00:25:31.667 --> 00:25:33.500
It's not worth these things.

461
00:25:35.792 --> 00:25:37.876
And outside.
in my parents' home

462
00:25:37.959 --> 00:25:40.042
in a comfortable house
in New Hampshire.

463
00:25:40.125 --> 00:25:42.375
I sort of had
to start processing.

464
00:25:42.459 --> 00:25:44.250
I was horrified to learn

465
00:25:44.334 --> 00:25:46.226
how much my friends and
family had done to help me.

466
00:25:46.250 --> 00:25:47.792
I was inspired
and I was horrified.

467
00:25:47.876 --> 00:25:51.500
It was a weird feeling of.
like. going to your own funeral.

468
00:25:54.626 --> 00:25:57.083
There's no going back
from something like this.

469
00:25:59.542 --> 00:26:01.185
Some of the things
I'll never be able to change.

470
00:26:01.209 --> 00:26:03.459
but I wish...
I wish that I could.

471
00:26:13.209 --> 00:26:14.834
- This is...
- What are you gonna do?

472
00:26:14.918 --> 00:26:16.894
This is Jim's blazer from
when he spoke at Marquette.

473
00:26:16.918 --> 00:26:18.000
this brown one.

474
00:26:20.459 --> 00:26:22.709
Bunch of stuff
to go through.

475
00:26:24.334 --> 00:26:27.375
Yeah. so Jim lived with us
for. what. three months?

476
00:26:27.459 --> 00:26:30.209
Summer after he was
released from Libya.

477
00:26:30.292 --> 00:26:34.417
Summer until... yeah.
up until he went back.

478
00:26:34.500 --> 00:26:36.209
Used to have all
his clothes in here.

479
00:26:36.292 --> 00:26:39.626
You can still see his
CamelBak from the field.

480
00:26:39.709 --> 00:26:42.334
- Sleeping bags.
- All his sleeping bags.

481
00:26:42.417 --> 00:26:44.167
Yeah.

482
00:26:44.250 --> 00:26:45.834
That was his bed
right there.

483
00:26:45.918 --> 00:26:49.375
He'd always
crash out there.

484
00:26:49.459 --> 00:26:51.167
So it was cool
having Jim here.

485
00:26:51.250 --> 00:26:53.375
especially after him
having been gone in Libya.

486
00:26:53.459 --> 00:26:56.918
because I think
when he came back.

487
00:26:57.000 --> 00:26:59.500
you just wanted
to touch him a lot.

488
00:26:59.584 --> 00:27:02.626
Because. I don't know.
for me it was like. poke.

489
00:27:02.709 --> 00:27:04.042
and just make sure
he's real.

490
00:27:04.125 --> 00:27:05.542
It was almost...

491
00:27:05.626 --> 00:27:07.334
You're just
more appreciative of him.

492
00:27:07.417 --> 00:27:10.125
especially with the kids
getting to have him here

493
00:27:10.209 --> 00:27:13.000
because he was
always on the go.

494
00:27:13.083 --> 00:27:15.125
They loved their Uncle Jim.

495
00:27:15.209 --> 00:27:18.459
They'd come downstairs at the
crack of dawn and wake him up.

496
00:27:18.542 --> 00:27:21.709
It was good
as much as it...

497
00:27:21.792 --> 00:27:23.918
I think he had an itch
he couldn't scratch

498
00:27:24.000 --> 00:27:25.751
when he was domesticated.

499
00:27:30.042 --> 00:27:31.834
When Jim came back
from Libya.

500
00:27:31.918 --> 00:27:35.792
I offered him a full-time
job here as an editor.

501
00:27:35.876 --> 00:27:40.834
while he sorted out
what he would do next.

502
00:27:40.918 --> 00:27:45.250
He sat right outside
my office.

503
00:27:45.334 --> 00:27:47.667
He was grateful
to have the job.

504
00:27:47.751 --> 00:27:50.417
but working in an office

505
00:27:50.500 --> 00:27:55.125
was clearly not
what he liked the most.

506
00:27:55.209 --> 00:27:58.250
He was quiet.

507
00:27:58.334 --> 00:28:01.167
I don't want to say
he was withdrawn.

508
00:28:01.250 --> 00:28:03.250
but he was quiet.

509
00:28:05.167 --> 00:28:07.042
And I just remember Brad...

510
00:28:07.125 --> 00:28:09.876
My fiancé... and Jim
were in the basement.

511
00:28:09.959 --> 00:28:13.209
and Brad doesn't
even remember a time

512
00:28:13.292 --> 00:28:14.792
where Jim was sleeping.

513
00:28:14.876 --> 00:28:17.792
After the family
had kind of settled down.

514
00:28:17.876 --> 00:28:20.751
Jim just went right to
business. right to work.

515
00:28:20.834 --> 00:28:25.375
We had found him a very good
psychologist to talk to.

516
00:28:25.459 --> 00:28:28.334
but he seemed so well.
if you will.

517
00:28:28.417 --> 00:28:30.292
that we didn't push it.

518
00:28:30.375 --> 00:28:32.834
But he was so restless
here at home.

519
00:28:32.918 --> 00:28:34.626
He didn't want to
be here at home.

520
00:28:38.209 --> 00:28:40.125
Feeling like you
survived something.

521
00:28:40.209 --> 00:28:42.125
it has a strange
sort of force

522
00:28:42.209 --> 00:28:45.167
that you are
drawn back to.

523
00:28:45.250 --> 00:28:47.542
I think that's
the absolute reality.

524
00:28:52.000 --> 00:28:54.167
Everything else
becomes foreign.

525
00:28:54.250 --> 00:28:57.292
It was strange coming back
to the real world.

526
00:28:57.375 --> 00:29:01.000
It was weird.
It was normal in there.

527
00:29:01.083 --> 00:29:03.626
Yeah. if I go to Wal-Mart
and I'm looking at

528
00:29:03.709 --> 00:29:07.918
literally an entire aisle
full of Tupperware.

529
00:29:08.000 --> 00:29:09.459
it boggles my mind.

530
00:29:09.542 --> 00:29:13.042
There's a deep absurdity

531
00:29:13.125 --> 00:29:17.042
to understanding the peace

532
00:29:17.125 --> 00:29:20.167
that we're lucky enough
to have in this country

533
00:29:20.250 --> 00:29:22.626
and the questions
that it raises

534
00:29:22.709 --> 00:29:25.167
about how do
we make our money.

535
00:29:25.250 --> 00:29:27.542
what is the basis
of our peace.

536
00:29:27.626 --> 00:29:29.876
of our economic viability.

537
00:29:29.959 --> 00:29:33.584
of the fact that I can look
at all of this Tupperware

538
00:29:33.667 --> 00:29:36.292
and someone else
somewhere else

539
00:29:36.375 --> 00:29:38.626
is looking at
the ruins of their home.

540
00:29:40.959 --> 00:29:42.459
You start missing
all the bombs

541
00:29:42.542 --> 00:29:44.417
and the fighting
and everything.

542
00:29:44.500 --> 00:29:46.167
I just used to love
sitting up at night

543
00:29:46.250 --> 00:29:47.959
and just listening.

544
00:29:50.292 --> 00:29:51.834
It's really kind of sick.

545
00:29:51.918 --> 00:29:54.167
But it also...

546
00:29:54.250 --> 00:29:57.167
It feels super fortunate
for being there.

547
00:29:57.250 --> 00:30:00.375
You think. like. out of 25
million people in your country.

548
00:30:00.459 --> 00:30:03.500
you're the only person
who's got to experience that.

549
00:30:03.584 --> 00:30:05.876
Like. fuck. man.
how fortunate am I?

550
00:30:05.959 --> 00:30:07.584
You're witnessing
history unfolding

551
00:30:07.667 --> 00:30:10.709
and you're getting
this perspective

552
00:30:10.792 --> 00:30:13.292
that is so unique.

553
00:31:06.292 --> 00:31:09.417
I believe that frontline
journalism is important.

554
00:31:09.500 --> 00:31:12.083
Without these photos
and videos

555
00:31:12.167 --> 00:31:14.042
and firsthand experience.

556
00:31:14.125 --> 00:31:17.417
we can't really
tell the world

557
00:31:17.500 --> 00:31:19.584
how bad it might be.

558
00:31:34.542 --> 00:31:36.334
James Foley
joins us now live

559
00:31:36.417 --> 00:31:38.334
from inside northern Syria

560
00:31:38.417 --> 00:31:40.375
with more on what he saw.

561
00:31:40.459 --> 00:31:43.584
Tell us more about what
you were able to witness.

562
00:31:43.667 --> 00:31:45.083
Yes. thank you.

563
00:31:45.167 --> 00:31:47.083
You've heard about
indiscriminate shelling.

564
00:31:47.167 --> 00:31:49.792
but to see
those bodies left over

565
00:31:49.876 --> 00:31:53.083
from a direct mortar hit
was really shocking.

566
00:31:53.167 --> 00:31:54.667
These were civilians.

567
00:31:54.751 --> 00:31:57.542
and they are under pretty
continuous shelling.

568
00:32:12.500 --> 00:32:13.626
Thank you.

569
00:32:17.083 --> 00:32:21.959
It seemed like he started
thinking about going to Syria.

570
00:32:22.042 --> 00:32:24.626
and by the time
he mentioned it.

571
00:32:24.709 --> 00:32:27.918
it was like he'd already
kind of made up his mind.

572
00:32:29.250 --> 00:32:31.834
And he said he was going
with John Cantlie.

573
00:32:31.918 --> 00:32:34.876
who's another colleague...
A British photojournalist

574
00:32:34.959 --> 00:32:38.709
that we'd all met
in Libya the year before.

575
00:32:38.792 --> 00:32:40.918
- What's your name?
- Jim.

576
00:32:41.042 --> 00:32:43.542
- John. Jim and John.
- John. Jim.

577
00:32:43.626 --> 00:32:47.042
Johnny. you say I love you.
You say.

578
00:32:47.125 --> 00:32:48.709
I don't know.

579
00:32:48.792 --> 00:32:51.042
I really didn't

580
00:32:51.125 --> 00:32:53.959
really get
into it with Jim.

581
00:32:54.042 --> 00:32:56.209
I think I made it
too easy for him.

582
00:32:56.292 --> 00:32:59.375
I mean. it was something
he wanted to do.

583
00:32:59.459 --> 00:33:02.209
and so we were
trying to be supportive

584
00:33:02.292 --> 00:33:05.959
about his decision
to do that. you know?

585
00:33:06.042 --> 00:33:08.500
You just want to
punch him in the face.

586
00:33:08.584 --> 00:33:12.167
You know. in a loving.
brotherly way.

587
00:33:12.250 --> 00:33:15.042
But you're like.
"Come on. Jim! Come on!"

588
00:33:17.083 --> 00:33:19.667
The last conversation I ever
had with Jim. I said to him.

589
00:33:19.751 --> 00:33:22.209
"Jim. man. why do you
keep going back into Syria?

590
00:33:22.292 --> 00:33:23.918
I mean. what's it like?"

591
00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:25.584
And he's like.
"It's crazy. it's crazy."

592
00:33:25.667 --> 00:33:28.000
I'm like "Well. is it more
dangerous than Libya?"

593
00:33:28.083 --> 00:33:29.643
He's like. "Yeah. it's more
dangerous than Libya!"

594
00:33:29.667 --> 00:33:31.626
I'm like.
"You got captured in Libya!"

595
00:33:33.000 --> 00:33:35.125
You couldn't
talk him out of it.

596
00:34:15.542 --> 00:34:18.417
You know. the thing is. is
there's physical courage. right?

597
00:34:18.500 --> 00:34:20.876
For some reason.
I have physical courage.

598
00:34:20.959 --> 00:34:22.459
But really. think about it.

599
00:34:22.542 --> 00:34:24.459
that's nothing
compared to moral courage.

600
00:34:24.542 --> 00:34:26.375
I can go
and get those shots.

601
00:34:26.459 --> 00:34:28.292
but if I don't have
the moral courage

602
00:34:28.375 --> 00:34:31.751
to challenge authority.
to write about things

603
00:34:31.834 --> 00:34:35.125
that are going to maybe have
reprisals on my career...

604
00:34:35.209 --> 00:34:37.959
If I don't have moral courage.
we don't have journalism.

605
00:34:40.125 --> 00:34:43.209
Jim chose one story in
particular about this hospital.

606
00:34:43.292 --> 00:34:45.709
the Dar Al Shifa
Hospital in Aleppo.

607
00:34:45.792 --> 00:34:49.500
It was actually Jim's idea to
spend a week in that hospital

608
00:34:49.584 --> 00:34:52.500
documenting what the doctors
and the staff there

609
00:34:52.584 --> 00:34:54.542
were doing
on a daily basis.

610
00:34:56.751 --> 00:34:58.209
Yes.

611
00:35:14.709 --> 00:35:16.292
From what he
and I witnessed.

612
00:35:16.375 --> 00:35:18.459
they were literally
shuttling people with drips

613
00:35:18.542 --> 00:35:20.292
and bandages
and everything

614
00:35:20.375 --> 00:35:23.626
in these tiny
little taxis and cars.

615
00:35:23.709 --> 00:35:26.000
And Jim was the one
who came up with the idea

616
00:35:26.083 --> 00:35:29.083
of raising money to get an
ambulance for the hospital.

617
00:35:29.167 --> 00:35:30.751
And he was in touch
with everybody.

618
00:35:30.834 --> 00:35:32.375
and there was
an ambulance

619
00:35:32.459 --> 00:35:33.709
that was
a secondhand ambulance

620
00:35:33.792 --> 00:35:35.542
that was coming
from Austria.

621
00:35:35.626 --> 00:35:37.667
and Jim and I actually
made one trip in

622
00:35:37.751 --> 00:35:39.876
and we saw the ambulance
sitting outside the hospital.

623
00:35:39.959 --> 00:35:44.292
And that moment of joy
on his face was priceless.

624
00:35:44.375 --> 00:35:46.626
He was just like.
"It made it. It's awesome."

625
00:35:49.918 --> 00:35:53.500
I would say the flip side
of his willingness

626
00:35:53.584 --> 00:35:55.417
to get out there
and do things

627
00:35:55.500 --> 00:35:59.125
is that like. okay. so you
get an ambulance into Syria.

628
00:35:59.209 --> 00:36:00.876
Some militia is
going to commandeer it

629
00:36:00.959 --> 00:36:03.000
and use it
for their own purposes.

630
00:36:03.083 --> 00:36:04.727
They're going to put an
anti-aircraft in the back of it.

631
00:36:04.751 --> 00:36:06.083
like. go blow shit up.

632
00:36:06.167 --> 00:36:10.375
Jim was not focused
on that kind of issue.

633
00:36:10.459 --> 00:36:14.542
He would think about what's
the best-case scenario.

634
00:36:14.626 --> 00:36:17.500
not how things
could go wrong.

635
00:36:20.876 --> 00:36:22.709
It becomes very personal

636
00:36:22.792 --> 00:36:25.918
because we have to live
amongst the local population.

637
00:36:26.000 --> 00:36:29.918
and it's such this brutal.
endless conflict

638
00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:32.083
that you just...

639
00:36:32.167 --> 00:36:35.292
You feel so kind of alone

640
00:36:35.375 --> 00:36:37.375
in the sense that you
can't do anything about it.

641
00:36:37.459 --> 00:36:39.709
You can't do anything
for these people...

642
00:36:39.792 --> 00:36:41.542
For the people
you've made friends with

643
00:36:41.626 --> 00:36:43.167
and for the people
who took you in

644
00:36:43.250 --> 00:36:45.000
and shared
their food with you

645
00:36:45.083 --> 00:36:47.876
and wanted you to
play with their children.

646
00:36:47.959 --> 00:36:50.542
We'd become the intimate
chroniclers of this conflict.

647
00:36:50.626 --> 00:36:52.417
We don't have bureaus
to go back to.

648
00:36:52.500 --> 00:36:54.834
You're there.
and every moment of it

649
00:36:54.918 --> 00:36:57.876
you share with the locals.

650
00:36:57.959 --> 00:37:01.626
And I think there was just this
enormous guilt that rode on Jim's back

651
00:37:01.709 --> 00:37:04.751
that made him
feel so compelled

652
00:37:04.834 --> 00:37:08.918
to do much more than
just record video and file it.

653
00:37:09.000 --> 00:37:11.667
There were times where he was
offering up video for free

654
00:37:11.751 --> 00:37:14.459
and I would chastise him for it.
I'd be like. "What are you doing?"

655
00:37:14.542 --> 00:37:17.250
He was like. "Nah. whatever.
It's fine. It's all good.

656
00:37:17.334 --> 00:37:19.876
I just want to make sure
the video gets out there."

657
00:37:23.209 --> 00:37:27.083
Jim's the kind of guy
who never needed much.

658
00:37:27.167 --> 00:37:31.375
Some of the lifestyle of a
conflict journalist is tough.

659
00:37:31.459 --> 00:37:33.959
but that didn't bother Jim.

660
00:37:34.042 --> 00:37:35.959
The only possessions
I think Jim cared about

661
00:37:36.042 --> 00:37:37.709
were books and CDs.

662
00:37:37.792 --> 00:37:39.667
I mean his camera.
ultimately.

663
00:37:39.751 --> 00:37:41.792
He'd come home
without a toothbrush.

664
00:37:41.876 --> 00:37:45.167
Just use whatever
toothbrush was available.

665
00:37:45.250 --> 00:37:47.417
Jim could fall asleep
anywhere.

666
00:37:47.500 --> 00:37:51.209
All he needed was just
a little space on the floor.

667
00:37:51.292 --> 00:37:52.500
He was like a cat.

668
00:37:54.209 --> 00:37:57.209
This is a good one.

669
00:38:05.709 --> 00:38:07.667
Over time. you know.

670
00:38:07.751 --> 00:38:09.334
he slowly got rid
of his apartment.

671
00:38:09.417 --> 00:38:10.751
sold his car.

672
00:38:10.834 --> 00:38:15.042
He just ended up
really owning nothing.

673
00:38:15.125 --> 00:38:16.709
So. what would
he want for Christmas?

674
00:38:16.792 --> 00:38:18.334
This last time
before going to Syria.

675
00:38:18.417 --> 00:38:20.292
he wanted a tough
pair of pants.

676
00:38:20.375 --> 00:38:21.876
He really did
have less and less

677
00:38:21.959 --> 00:38:24.125
and it didn't
bother him at all

678
00:38:24.209 --> 00:38:25.876
because he seemed
richer and richer.

679
00:38:38.667 --> 00:38:40.709
There was one day
in August

680
00:38:40.792 --> 00:38:43.792
when a Syrian activist was
taking us around this neighborhood

681
00:38:43.876 --> 00:38:46.250
called Bustan al-Qasr

682
00:38:46.334 --> 00:38:50.584
and this fighter jet
just started circling above

683
00:38:50.667 --> 00:38:53.500
and just swooped right down
and hit a building

684
00:38:53.584 --> 00:38:55.667
that was a couple hundred
feet from us.

685
00:39:01.459 --> 00:39:03.834
We started seeing
civilians coming out

686
00:39:03.918 --> 00:39:07.250
and just clutching
nothing. really.

687
00:39:07.334 --> 00:39:08.626
Just ashen faces.

688
00:39:08.709 --> 00:39:10.918
There was rubble everywhere.

689
00:39:11.000 --> 00:39:12.209
It was chaos.

690
00:39:16.334 --> 00:39:17.918
And the plane
came around again.

691
00:39:18.000 --> 00:39:20.042
It dropped another bomb
really close by.

692
00:39:20.125 --> 00:39:22.125
and. actually. we were
right across the building.

693
00:39:22.209 --> 00:39:24.125
and we looked up
and we could see

694
00:39:24.209 --> 00:39:26.417
the rubble start
coming down.

695
00:39:31.209 --> 00:39:36.209
That bomb had hit
a family of seven.

696
00:39:41.918 --> 00:39:43.083
Who was killed?

697
00:39:44.209 --> 00:39:45.709
Who?

698
00:39:49.626 --> 00:39:51.584
It was horrific in
the scale of it.

699
00:39:51.667 --> 00:39:54.292
but also just...
I think nothing prepares you

700
00:39:54.375 --> 00:39:58.042
for seeing kids being killed
and maimed in that way.

701
00:39:58.125 --> 00:40:00.375
and I know that Jim
really loves kids.

702
00:40:00.459 --> 00:40:02.834
So. you know.
we were both just...

703
00:40:02.918 --> 00:40:06.918
We didn't say anything until
we got to the field hospital

704
00:40:07.000 --> 00:40:08.459
where they were
bringing the bodies.

705
00:40:14.834 --> 00:40:17.918
And we were both
just in this mode

706
00:40:18.000 --> 00:40:20.792
of just needing to get
the pictures out.

707
00:40:20.876 --> 00:40:23.250
I think when we were
finished that night. though.

708
00:40:23.334 --> 00:40:25.834
we kind of sat down
and lit a cigarette

709
00:40:25.918 --> 00:40:28.626
and we just started
talking about it.

710
00:40:28.709 --> 00:40:30.792
Really. there wasn't
very much to say. though.

711
00:40:30.876 --> 00:40:33.834
You know. like.
what is there to talk about

712
00:40:33.918 --> 00:40:36.250
when you witness
something like that?

713
00:40:36.334 --> 00:40:38.250
So we just sort of
sat in silence.

714
00:40:40.500 --> 00:40:42.417
Nothing prepares
you for that.

715
00:40:42.500 --> 00:40:44.667
No amount of courses.
Nothing.

716
00:40:44.751 --> 00:40:48.667
You just go there and you'll
either handle it or you don't.

717
00:40:48.751 --> 00:40:50.334
and that's cool.

718
00:40:50.417 --> 00:40:52.918
Either you run
or you stay.

719
00:40:53.000 --> 00:40:55.834
And there's... not one
is better than the other.

720
00:40:55.918 --> 00:40:58.167
but just don't
delude yourself.

721
00:40:58.250 --> 00:41:00.709
Some people aren't
meant for that.

722
00:41:11.125 --> 00:41:14.876
The shine was starting
to come off in a way.

723
00:41:14.959 --> 00:41:17.000
There was a period of time

724
00:41:17.083 --> 00:41:19.209
where journalists
were welcomed with open arms

725
00:41:19.292 --> 00:41:22.209
because they'd seen
what had happened in Libya.

726
00:41:22.292 --> 00:41:25.000
And when that didn't
come about after a year.

727
00:41:25.083 --> 00:41:27.751
after a year and a half.
after two years.

728
00:41:27.834 --> 00:41:29.714
it's just like. "Okay.
what are you guys doing?"

729
00:41:29.751 --> 00:41:32.334
I had a doctor
tell me at the hospital

730
00:41:32.417 --> 00:41:34.667
that Jim helped raise money
for an ambulance...

731
00:41:34.751 --> 00:41:38.000
He was like. "Look. you guys are
in and out of here since one year.

732
00:41:38.083 --> 00:41:40.584
and it's the same exact thing
except it's worse.

733
00:41:40.667 --> 00:41:42.918
I don't want to
talk to you."

734
00:41:45.834 --> 00:41:47.918
If the populace
on the ground

735
00:41:48.000 --> 00:41:50.209
whose side
you're documenting

736
00:41:50.292 --> 00:41:53.584
is getting
more uneasy with you

737
00:41:53.667 --> 00:41:56.292
or less willing to help...

738
00:41:56.375 --> 00:41:57.918
You're very dependent
on the goodwill

739
00:41:58.000 --> 00:41:59.709
of the people
you're around.

740
00:42:28.584 --> 00:42:33.000
It's just that moment of. like.
how well can you know anybody?

741
00:42:33.083 --> 00:42:36.584
Even. say you know
someone very well.

742
00:42:36.667 --> 00:42:38.334
two years of war.
three years of war.

743
00:42:38.417 --> 00:42:40.876
that's going
to change anybody.

744
00:42:40.959 --> 00:42:42.209
They warned journalists.

745
00:42:42.292 --> 00:42:44.125
They were like.
"Al-Qaeda is coming."

746
00:42:44.209 --> 00:42:45.876
Maybe even worse
than al-Qaeda is coming

747
00:42:45.959 --> 00:42:49.167
and nobody is going to help us
against the Assad regime

748
00:42:49.250 --> 00:42:51.792
except for these guys.

749
00:42:51.876 --> 00:42:53.626
So it was all there.

750
00:42:57.584 --> 00:42:59.751
- One of the main things.
- I noticed

751
00:42:59.834 --> 00:43:01.959
the last time
when he came out.

752
00:43:02.042 --> 00:43:03.250
he looked really hollow

753
00:43:03.334 --> 00:43:05.834
and he was quite...
Quite silent.

754
00:43:05.918 --> 00:43:10.125
He had that amazing
room-brightening smile

755
00:43:10.209 --> 00:43:14.083
even if he had seen
terrible things. as one does.

756
00:43:14.167 --> 00:43:16.000
It was disheartening
to see.

757
00:43:20.709 --> 00:43:23.918
- Before he left for Syria.
- I think we made it a point.

758
00:43:24.000 --> 00:43:27.000
we were going to bring him down
to the Faneuil Hall district

759
00:43:27.083 --> 00:43:29.792
and go to the comedy club
down there.

760
00:43:29.876 --> 00:43:31.167
The comedians
were horrible.

761
00:43:31.250 --> 00:43:32.751
but I was in
the mood to laugh.

762
00:43:32.834 --> 00:43:34.626
so I'm just
laughing at anything.

763
00:43:34.709 --> 00:43:36.209
And I just remember
looking at Jim

764
00:43:36.292 --> 00:43:38.500
and he was just
dead-faced.

765
00:43:38.584 --> 00:43:42.000
And then me and Jim
went outside for a cigarette

766
00:43:42.083 --> 00:43:44.042
like we always do.

767
00:43:44.125 --> 00:43:45.459
He said he had to go.

768
00:43:45.542 --> 00:43:47.125
We had a long hug.

769
00:43:47.209 --> 00:43:49.709
I remember I hugged him
a really long time.

770
00:43:49.792 --> 00:43:50.959
Just hugged him
really tight.

771
00:43:51.042 --> 00:43:52.792
I said be careful.

772
00:43:52.876 --> 00:43:54.643
Obviously. I didn't feel
like that was the last time

773
00:43:54.667 --> 00:43:58.000
I was going to see him.
but it was a good night.

774
00:43:58.083 --> 00:43:59.683
It was a good end
of the night. you know?

775
00:44:02.834 --> 00:44:05.459
John. Sr.: I guess if I
had any regrets. Brian.

776
00:44:05.542 --> 00:44:09.209
I regret that I found it difficult
to communicate with Jim.

777
00:44:09.292 --> 00:44:12.584
I don't know if it's the
male thing or whatever it is.

778
00:44:12.667 --> 00:44:15.209
but I just wish
I was able to share more

779
00:44:15.292 --> 00:44:16.959
of who I was
with Jim again

780
00:44:17.042 --> 00:44:18.751
and get him to share
who he was with me.

781
00:44:18.834 --> 00:44:21.751
which might have been
just as difficult. you know?

782
00:44:21.834 --> 00:44:24.500
He would interview us
when we came home

783
00:44:24.584 --> 00:44:26.500
and he did a great job
interviewing us.

784
00:44:26.584 --> 00:44:30.375
because you felt like talking
because he was listening.

785
00:44:30.459 --> 00:44:32.500
He wanted to know
how we were.

786
00:44:32.584 --> 00:44:33.852
And that was...
When he came home.

787
00:44:33.876 --> 00:44:35.459
that's what
he wanted to know.

788
00:44:35.542 --> 00:44:39.042
He wanted. "Well. how
are you? How you doing?"

789
00:44:39.125 --> 00:44:41.959
"How's Grandma?
How's Katie and Mark?"

790
00:44:42.042 --> 00:44:45.083
And he just wanted to know
how everyone was doing.

791
00:44:45.167 --> 00:44:49.000
So in that way.
Jim was kind of solitary.

792
00:44:51.125 --> 00:44:53.125
He was home.
end of October.

793
00:44:53.209 --> 00:44:56.542
right before he went back
to Syria that last time.

794
00:44:56.626 --> 00:44:58.500
and he was going...

795
00:44:58.584 --> 00:44:59.834
I remember
he was leaving here

796
00:44:59.918 --> 00:45:01.560
and he was going to
New York to get a helmet

797
00:45:01.584 --> 00:45:03.542
from somebody.
which was good.

798
00:45:03.626 --> 00:45:06.375
We were like. "Getting
safety equipment. We like this."

799
00:45:06.459 --> 00:45:10.417
Yeah. I remember we left and we
dropped him off at a train station.

800
00:45:10.500 --> 00:45:13.000
And he... we were going
to see him in December.

801
00:45:13.083 --> 00:45:15.393
- He was supposed to come home kind of...
- You have a good memory.

802
00:45:15.417 --> 00:45:17.057
He was supposed to
come home mid-December.

803
00:45:17.125 --> 00:45:21.125
And we were like.
"Be safe. See you soon."

804
00:45:21.209 --> 00:45:24.876
And unfortunately.
that didn't...

805
00:45:24.959 --> 00:45:27.042
Yeah.

806
00:45:32.042 --> 00:45:34.584
- Sorry.
- It's okay.

807
00:45:49.209 --> 00:45:52.417
We'd spent the beginning
of November in Aleppo again

808
00:45:52.500 --> 00:45:56.834
with Jim. John Cantlie.
and Mustafa. our translator.

809
00:45:56.918 --> 00:45:59.125
who's become
a friend of ours.

810
00:45:59.209 --> 00:46:02.500
I had had some issues
with my camera that week.

811
00:46:02.584 --> 00:46:06.000
So. I was like. "Jim. I got
to go back to Istanbul.

812
00:46:06.083 --> 00:46:08.500
I'll see you guys
in a week."

813
00:46:08.584 --> 00:46:12.918
The moment when
I said bye to Jim.

814
00:46:13.000 --> 00:46:17.292
I had this feeling
of reluctance to leave.

815
00:46:17.375 --> 00:46:20.542
I think in a way maybe
it did upset the balance

816
00:46:20.626 --> 00:46:23.167
that he and I
had shared for so long.

817
00:46:23.250 --> 00:46:26.209
There are superstitions
when you're in a war zone.

818
00:46:29.918 --> 00:46:32.500
There's like this one thing
that he and I shared.

819
00:46:32.584 --> 00:46:34.125
which was our
lucky lighter.

820
00:46:34.209 --> 00:46:35.959
It's very common
in the Middle East.

821
00:46:36.042 --> 00:46:39.042
It's like the evil eye
to ward off evil spirits.

822
00:46:39.125 --> 00:46:41.000
We'd used it
for everything.

823
00:46:41.083 --> 00:46:43.101
and for some reason it never
ran out of lighter fluid.

824
00:46:43.125 --> 00:46:44.959
It's just this stupid idea.

825
00:46:45.042 --> 00:46:49.918
You put your hopes into one
object to make it feel safe.

826
00:46:51.334 --> 00:46:53.584
I think about it
a lot afterwards.

827
00:46:53.667 --> 00:46:56.375
that he didn't
have it with him.

828
00:46:56.459 --> 00:47:00.792
Maybe if I just gave him
the lucky lighter.

829
00:47:00.876 --> 00:47:02.751
everything might have
turned out okay.

830
00:47:02.834 --> 00:47:04.250
I don't know.

831
00:47:06.709 --> 00:47:09.375
That day.
I was in Reyhanli.

832
00:47:09.459 --> 00:47:11.125
which is the border town.

833
00:47:11.209 --> 00:47:14.250
and I would have
seen him at about 5:00.

834
00:47:14.334 --> 00:47:16.792
So I checked in
and I told Jim.

835
00:47:16.876 --> 00:47:18.918
I'm like.
"Hey. I'm here.

836
00:47:19.000 --> 00:47:21.584
so text me
when you get in."

837
00:47:22.959 --> 00:47:26.667
5:00 rolls by.
and I started to worry.

838
00:47:26.751 --> 00:47:28.792
7:00.
8:00 rolls around.

839
00:47:28.876 --> 00:47:30.516
and I'm like.
"Something is really wrong."

840
00:47:30.584 --> 00:47:33.375
So I called Mustafa.

841
00:47:33.459 --> 00:47:36.542
and the first thing
he said to me was.

842
00:47:36.626 --> 00:47:39.584
"Nicole. I'm so sorry.

843
00:47:39.667 --> 00:47:41.542
I didn't...
I couldn't do anything."

844
00:47:41.626 --> 00:47:43.768
I was like. "What are you
talking about? What happened?"

845
00:47:43.792 --> 00:47:46.959
He was like. "We were
coming. We were in the taxi.

846
00:47:47.042 --> 00:47:48.751
We were coming
to Turkey to meet you.

847
00:47:48.834 --> 00:47:53.209
and this van with
these four guys with guns.

848
00:47:53.292 --> 00:47:55.042
they stopped us
on the road

849
00:47:55.125 --> 00:47:56.918
and they told us
to get out.

850
00:47:57.000 --> 00:48:00.459
And they were pointing their
guns at us and screaming."

851
00:48:00.542 --> 00:48:04.626
The gunman made Mustafa
tie up their hands.

852
00:48:04.709 --> 00:48:08.584
and they put John and Jim
into the back of their van.

853
00:48:10.667 --> 00:48:14.375
So. I hung up
and I just started crying.

854
00:48:14.459 --> 00:48:16.042
I was like...

855
00:48:20.167 --> 00:48:21.810
I didn't know if I was
gonna see Jim again.

856
00:48:21.834 --> 00:48:24.626
and that was the first thought
that came to my head.

857
00:48:30.542 --> 00:48:32.584
John. Sr.:
It was almost surreal.

858
00:48:32.667 --> 00:48:35.459
This is a bad dream.
It's not really happening.

859
00:48:35.542 --> 00:48:38.918
It's not happening.
This can't be...

860
00:48:39.000 --> 00:48:40.167
This can't happen again.

861
00:48:40.250 --> 00:48:42.500
We can't do this again.

862
00:48:45.792 --> 00:48:48.000
Yeah. it was...

863
00:48:48.083 --> 00:48:50.167
I dove in just like before.

864
00:48:50.250 --> 00:48:53.334
I felt like. "Okay. it's going
to be 45 to 100 days of hell.

865
00:48:53.417 --> 00:48:55.834
and then
we'll have him back."

866
00:48:55.918 --> 00:48:58.876
In a matter of a week. you
could tell it was very different.

867
00:48:58.959 --> 00:49:01.834
A lot of misinformation. as
opposed to last time in Libya...

868
00:49:01.918 --> 00:49:05.834
After a week went by we knew
where he was. who to deal with.

869
00:49:05.918 --> 00:49:08.375
so we just concentrated on
routes to get to one person.

870
00:49:08.459 --> 00:49:11.792
Here was a mystery right out
of a crime show or something

871
00:49:11.876 --> 00:49:15.500
where you're trying to piece
together bits of information.

872
00:49:15.584 --> 00:49:17.375
For the next three weeks.

873
00:49:17.459 --> 00:49:20.459
there was just dead ends
and false information

874
00:49:20.542 --> 00:49:23.250
and rumors and people
being scared of talking

875
00:49:23.334 --> 00:49:27.542
because they had a suspicion
of who maybe was responsible

876
00:49:27.626 --> 00:49:29.459
and they didn't want to
get entangled in it.

877
00:49:29.542 --> 00:49:33.709
Phil Balboni offered
to stand up

878
00:49:33.792 --> 00:49:35.918
a security team
to try to find Jim.

879
00:49:36.000 --> 00:49:38.626
So. all these people
were trying so hard

880
00:49:38.709 --> 00:49:41.375
to get in place.
but it was a very.

881
00:49:41.459 --> 00:49:43.375
very chaotic.
confusing time.

882
00:49:46.250 --> 00:49:50.083
John. Jr.: You're on eggshells.
You're just waiting to hear.

883
00:49:50.167 --> 00:49:52.876
It's exhausting. and I
know Jim felt guilty for that.

884
00:49:52.959 --> 00:49:56.626
and I'm not trying to
make him feel more guilty.

885
00:49:56.709 --> 00:49:58.542
but it's just a toll

886
00:49:58.626 --> 00:50:03.000
that's taken
by the families.

887
00:50:03.083 --> 00:50:05.000
What were the hurdles
getting the White House

888
00:50:05.083 --> 00:50:06.584
and FBI involved?

889
00:50:06.667 --> 00:50:09.000
It was very tough
to get action.

890
00:50:09.083 --> 00:50:11.250
And I understand that
the world is a big place.

891
00:50:11.334 --> 00:50:14.459
and so I actually
felt guilty sometimes

892
00:50:14.542 --> 00:50:17.375
trying not to ask
too much of them.

893
00:50:17.459 --> 00:50:18.584
Jim made this decision.

894
00:50:18.667 --> 00:50:21.083
but just give it
your best attention

895
00:50:21.167 --> 00:50:22.918
and we'll trust you.

896
00:50:23.000 --> 00:50:26.209
That's kind of where it
started. the relationship.

897
00:50:26.292 --> 00:50:29.209
Then I met the first agent
that came over

898
00:50:29.292 --> 00:50:33.042
and it was just a kid
out of school.

899
00:50:33.125 --> 00:50:36.876
and his first question to me was
had I asked the regime for assistance.

900
00:50:36.959 --> 00:50:38.709
Are you
fucking kidding me?

901
00:50:38.792 --> 00:50:41.542
Have I called the regime
and asked them for assistance?

902
00:50:41.626 --> 00:50:43.584
No. I hadn't thought of that.
Thank you.

903
00:50:43.667 --> 00:50:46.292
Thank you very much
for that tip.

904
00:50:46.375 --> 00:50:49.459
They told us... they
advised us to be quiet.

905
00:50:49.542 --> 00:50:52.334
because hopefully
they could find him

906
00:50:52.417 --> 00:50:53.792
and get him out and such.

907
00:50:53.876 --> 00:50:55.584
So we didn't say anything.

908
00:50:55.667 --> 00:50:58.292
We went through
Christmas and all that

909
00:50:58.375 --> 00:51:01.834
not telling anyone but our
closest family that Jim was missing.

910
00:51:04.209 --> 00:51:06.584
In some ways
it was better

911
00:51:06.667 --> 00:51:09.584
because I didn't
have to explain it.

912
00:51:09.667 --> 00:51:12.250
Because at times
it could feel accusatory.

913
00:51:12.334 --> 00:51:14.500
like. "Well. he was
in Syria."

914
00:51:14.584 --> 00:51:17.792
That's not fair.

915
00:51:17.876 --> 00:51:19.584
You don't do that
with police or firemen

916
00:51:19.667 --> 00:51:21.226
or something like that
who do dangerous jobs.

917
00:51:21.250 --> 00:51:22.560
You don't say.
"Well. you were in a fire.

918
00:51:22.584 --> 00:51:24.459
What did you think
would happen?"

919
00:51:24.542 --> 00:51:27.667
My friend doesn't need to
explain why he's a journalist.

920
00:51:29.834 --> 00:51:33.000
- Come the new year.
- I couldn't stand it.

921
00:51:33.083 --> 00:51:34.417
I was frantic.

922
00:51:34.500 --> 00:51:37.584
So we chose to go public.

923
00:51:37.667 --> 00:51:40.667
John. Sr.: I appeal to
the people who have Jim

924
00:51:40.751 --> 00:51:44.959
to give us some information in
terms of his welfare. his health.

925
00:51:45.042 --> 00:51:46.542
It breaks my heart

926
00:51:46.626 --> 00:51:50.292
that the persons
who have captured him

927
00:51:50.375 --> 00:51:53.709
don't understand
his goodness.

928
00:51:53.792 --> 00:51:57.751
My personal feeling is that
silence helps two people.

929
00:51:57.834 --> 00:51:59.250
One is the government...

930
00:51:59.334 --> 00:52:02.125
It doesn't push them
to do more sooner...

931
00:52:02.209 --> 00:52:03.542
And the captors.

932
00:52:03.626 --> 00:52:06.542
It allows them to do
whatever they want.

933
00:52:06.626 --> 00:52:08.250
It's difficult now

934
00:52:08.334 --> 00:52:11.209
with all the talk
about the Islamic State.

935
00:52:11.292 --> 00:52:15.334
They've become
so famous or infamous.

936
00:52:15.417 --> 00:52:18.334
But the Islamic State.
ISIS. ISIL.

937
00:52:18.417 --> 00:52:22.417
was on nobody's
radar at the time.

938
00:52:22.500 --> 00:52:24.584
With a very high
degree of confidence.

939
00:52:24.667 --> 00:52:27.918
we now believe
that Jim was abducted

940
00:52:28.000 --> 00:52:29.918
by a pro-government
militia group

941
00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:31.500
and was subsequently
turned over

942
00:52:31.584 --> 00:52:33.167
to Syrian
government forces.

943
00:52:33.250 --> 00:52:34.417
This is the first time

944
00:52:34.500 --> 00:52:36.500
we've really heard
anything like this.

945
00:52:36.584 --> 00:52:40.667
so we are very hopeful.
like John says.

946
00:52:40.751 --> 00:52:43.083
Well. it turned out
we were dead wrong about that.

947
00:52:45.375 --> 00:52:48.083
All the information up front
was just a bunch of BS.

948
00:52:48.167 --> 00:52:50.087
No one knew what the heck
they were talking about

949
00:52:50.167 --> 00:52:51.918
because we had no access.

950
00:52:52.000 --> 00:52:54.918
That whole year.
I don't care what anyone says.

951
00:52:55.000 --> 00:52:57.040
all the efforts. all the
leads. all this and that...

952
00:52:58.375 --> 00:53:01.542
we were in the wrong area
of the country. you know?

953
00:53:01.626 --> 00:53:03.542
Syria is about the size
of New England. maybe?

954
00:53:03.626 --> 00:53:05.584
A little smaller.
So here you go. Brian.

955
00:53:05.667 --> 00:53:09.167
Go into New England.
Find Jim.

956
00:53:09.250 --> 00:53:11.667
I think the moment that I learned
that he had been kidnapped.

957
00:53:11.751 --> 00:53:15.751
I was just like. this is going
to be a really long process.

958
00:53:15.834 --> 00:53:17.375
But I'm going
to do anything

959
00:53:17.459 --> 00:53:20.125
that I can in my power
to get him home.

960
00:53:20.209 --> 00:53:24.709
Because I can't stand
the thought of him

961
00:53:24.792 --> 00:53:28.042
being in a cell somewhere
cold and hungry.

962
00:53:28.125 --> 00:53:29.751
And I can't sit here

963
00:53:29.834 --> 00:53:31.626
and not try
to look for him.

964
00:53:49.375 --> 00:53:51.083
My name is Daniel.

965
00:53:51.167 --> 00:53:53.334
and I'm a Danish
photojournalist.

966
00:53:54.542 --> 00:53:57.584
I started as a gymnast.

967
00:53:57.667 --> 00:54:00.167
and while I spent
all my time in gyms

968
00:54:00.250 --> 00:54:02.751
all around the world
doing gymnastics.

969
00:54:02.834 --> 00:54:05.417
I got bored when I didn't do
anything else

970
00:54:05.500 --> 00:54:07.417
and I started to
take pictures.

971
00:54:11.125 --> 00:54:16.167
I only planned to be inside
Syria for two days.

972
00:54:16.250 --> 00:54:19.250
I had one day of work

973
00:54:19.334 --> 00:54:23.083
when I walked around
this small quiet town.

974
00:54:24.250 --> 00:54:27.083
It was spring.

975
00:54:27.167 --> 00:54:29.667
so the weather
started to be very better.

976
00:54:29.751 --> 00:54:33.667
and people seemed
happy and relaxed

977
00:54:33.751 --> 00:54:37.292
and we were told
to go and speak

978
00:54:37.375 --> 00:54:40.250
to some guys in the area.

979
00:54:42.709 --> 00:54:44.459
Very calmly.
we're sitting on sofas.

980
00:54:44.542 --> 00:54:47.250
They were offering tea.

981
00:54:47.334 --> 00:54:49.584
Everything
was calm and quiet.

982
00:54:49.667 --> 00:54:52.709
even though I knew that
something was strange.

983
00:54:52.792 --> 00:54:54.250
something was wrong.

984
00:54:54.334 --> 00:54:57.584
And then they
asked me to stand up

985
00:54:57.667 --> 00:54:59.584
and they took off
my glasses.

986
00:54:59.667 --> 00:55:03.918
and they just... "Don't worry.
this is just a procedure."

987
00:55:06.626 --> 00:55:08.042
That was how
everything began.

988
00:55:08.125 --> 00:55:10.167
So. you know.
a quiet Sunday

989
00:55:10.250 --> 00:55:13.959
beautiful spring morning
became a nightmare for me.

990
00:55:22.167 --> 00:55:24.042
The longer
you are a hostage.

991
00:55:24.125 --> 00:55:26.751
the easier
it becomes in some way.

992
00:55:26.834 --> 00:55:28.167
the better you get at it.

993
00:55:28.250 --> 00:55:30.083
And I had. like.
I think. one.

994
00:55:30.167 --> 00:55:32.250
one and half months
by myself.

995
00:55:32.334 --> 00:55:35.209
After. I believe.
two and a half months.

996
00:55:35.292 --> 00:55:38.918
I was put together
with two other Westerners.

997
00:55:39.000 --> 00:55:42.167
And then we were
put together four.

998
00:55:42.250 --> 00:55:43.667
I think we were
five together.

999
00:55:43.751 --> 00:55:45.083
and then seven together.

1000
00:55:45.167 --> 00:55:47.375
And then came
another one... eight.

1001
00:55:47.459 --> 00:55:49.459
Then it just started
to evolve.

1002
00:56:00.792 --> 00:56:02.375
We were in that cell...

1003
00:56:02.459 --> 00:56:05.167
British. Americans.

1004
00:56:05.250 --> 00:56:08.375
French. Italian. German.

1005
00:56:08.459 --> 00:56:11.209
Danish. Russian. Spanish.

1006
00:56:11.292 --> 00:56:13.334
We were 19 at one stage.

1007
00:57:54.125 --> 00:57:58.876
One day we had to sit
faces to the wall.

1008
00:57:58.959 --> 00:58:01.209
but I could see
under my arms.

1009
00:58:01.292 --> 00:58:04.375
I could see some
mattresses were moved in.

1010
00:58:04.459 --> 00:58:08.167
and there came some guys
in traditional Syrian clothes.

1011
00:58:08.250 --> 00:58:12.209
And then they closed the
door again. the big metal door.

1012
00:58:12.292 --> 00:58:15.834
and I looked up. and there
was James and John Cantlie.

1013
00:58:17.626 --> 00:58:19.876
Everybody was like. "Yay!
Welcome! Welcome! Welcome!"

1014
00:58:19.959 --> 00:58:22.334
It's two new friends.

1015
00:58:32.209 --> 00:58:35.125
I created this
picture in my head

1016
00:58:35.209 --> 00:58:37.918
of this big war
journalist.

1017
00:58:38.000 --> 00:58:41.125
and so I could only get
disappointed in a way.

1018
00:58:41.209 --> 00:58:43.834
But I remember
him being like...

1019
00:58:45.876 --> 00:58:47.375
"What's happening?"

1020
00:58:47.459 --> 00:58:50.083
So that was basically
the first time I met him.

1021
00:58:52.375 --> 00:58:53.918
But it was real different

1022
00:58:54.000 --> 00:58:56.375
to be put together
with James and John.

1023
00:58:56.459 --> 00:58:59.542
because they've been together
in prison for almost a year.

1024
00:58:59.626 --> 00:59:00.959
When I first saw them.

1025
00:59:01.042 --> 00:59:04.417
they were the most
experienced of us.

1026
00:59:04.500 --> 00:59:06.751
and I started
from the beginning.

1027
00:59:06.834 --> 00:59:11.000
I think the whole group started
to lean a little bit against them.

1028
00:59:58.417 --> 01:00:01.834
James was very silent
most of the time.

1029
01:00:01.918 --> 01:00:04.584
He was very good
at listening.

1030
01:00:04.667 --> 01:00:07.042
He managed to make
the room bigger in a way

1031
01:00:07.125 --> 01:00:09.292
by being small himself.

1032
01:00:09.375 --> 01:00:12.500
and that is a very.
very difficult thing to be.

1033
01:00:12.584 --> 01:00:15.751
And you really want to scream
in the head of everybody like.

1034
01:00:15.834 --> 01:00:17.876
"Fuck off!"

1035
01:01:33.959 --> 01:01:36.083
- I remember one time.
- James was asked

1036
01:01:36.167 --> 01:01:41.083
to stand up the whole night
in the middle of the room.

1037
01:02:52.167 --> 01:02:55.292
Late at night.
there was no light at all.

1038
01:02:55.375 --> 01:02:58.834
so we were just sitting there
in complete darkness.

1039
01:02:58.918 --> 01:03:03.542
and that time really.
really. really went slowly.

1040
01:03:03.626 --> 01:03:05.334
What we did.
James and I.

1041
01:03:05.417 --> 01:03:07.083
we started
to develop a way

1042
01:03:07.167 --> 01:03:11.500
of passing through
these hours of darkness

1043
01:03:11.584 --> 01:03:15.918
by giving each other.
like. massages.

1044
01:03:16.042 --> 01:03:19.626
And it sounds maybe
a little bit. you know.

1045
01:03:19.709 --> 01:03:23.042
strange or gay
or whatever.

1046
01:03:23.125 --> 01:03:27.375
but there was something...

1047
01:03:27.459 --> 01:03:30.417
There was something
nice about it.

1048
01:03:30.500 --> 01:03:32.834
And James asked me.
"Can you teach me

1049
01:03:32.918 --> 01:03:34.792
how to give
a real nice massage.

1050
01:03:34.876 --> 01:03:37.667
so when I get out
and I meet a woman. I can...

1051
01:03:37.751 --> 01:03:40.334
I can really impress her?"

1052
01:03:40.417 --> 01:03:45.000
So we started having
these kind of lessons.

1053
01:03:45.083 --> 01:03:48.292
Our bodies had witnessed
a lot of trauma

1054
01:03:48.375 --> 01:03:51.584
and the fact that somebody
is actually touching you

1055
01:03:51.667 --> 01:03:54.667
and it's a nice feeling.
for me.

1056
01:03:54.751 --> 01:03:58.542
it was a nice way to feel
a little bit human again.

1057
01:03:58.626 --> 01:04:01.959
And James. he never learned
how to give a proper massage.

1058
01:04:02.042 --> 01:04:03.250
It was awful every time.

1059
01:04:03.334 --> 01:04:05.876
So. he really...
He really managed

1060
01:04:05.959 --> 01:04:08.626
to get a good deal
out of that one.

1061
01:05:08.167 --> 01:05:09.626
I remember one time.

1062
01:05:09.709 --> 01:05:12.667
we were given
a lot of dates to eat.

1063
01:05:12.751 --> 01:05:15.042
And at some point
we were moved.

1064
01:05:15.125 --> 01:05:17.959
and you just don't
leave food behind

1065
01:05:18.042 --> 01:05:19.167
or destroy it or whatever.

1066
01:05:19.250 --> 01:05:20.667
but sometimes
you have to do it

1067
01:05:20.751 --> 01:05:22.876
because there's
nowhere to put it.

1068
01:05:42.834 --> 01:05:44.918
James. he just
took out his pants

1069
01:05:45.000 --> 01:05:46.959
and he took out
two kilos of dates.

1070
01:05:47.042 --> 01:05:49.209
"Don't worry. guys."

1071
01:05:51.918 --> 01:05:54.292
He could have taken
all the food by himself

1072
01:05:54.375 --> 01:05:55.959
later that night
or whatever.

1073
01:05:56.042 --> 01:05:57.584
but he always
took the things

1074
01:05:57.667 --> 01:05:59.083
so he could share
it around

1075
01:05:59.167 --> 01:06:00.887
or give it to the people
who didn't have it.

1076
01:07:16.751 --> 01:07:19.375
In the beginning of James
and John's captivity.

1077
01:07:19.459 --> 01:07:22.167
they were really starved.

1078
01:07:22.250 --> 01:07:24.083
They didn't like
to talk about it.

1079
01:07:24.167 --> 01:07:26.167
They didn't find it very
interesting to talk about.

1080
01:07:26.209 --> 01:07:29.292
But one thing
I know was that...

1081
01:07:32.167 --> 01:07:34.792
they really. really.
really had a difficult time.

1082
01:07:36.334 --> 01:07:39.918
But they managed
to get back on track

1083
01:07:40.000 --> 01:07:43.459
to gain strength again.

1084
01:07:43.542 --> 01:07:45.709
It was very
interesting to see

1085
01:07:45.792 --> 01:07:48.167
what happened
between James and John.

1086
01:07:48.250 --> 01:07:50.709
because they've been
together for almost a year

1087
01:07:50.792 --> 01:07:52.250
when I first saw them.

1088
01:07:52.334 --> 01:07:54.417
That meant
they had spoken about

1089
01:07:54.500 --> 01:07:57.542
every single thing
there is to talk about.

1090
01:07:57.626 --> 01:07:59.167
So I was basically the one

1091
01:07:59.250 --> 01:08:03.459
starting to listen
to all James' stories again.

1092
01:08:07.459 --> 01:08:09.918
There was a period
of time in the prison

1093
01:08:10.000 --> 01:08:12.167
where we were
not interrupted

1094
01:08:12.250 --> 01:08:14.167
by the guards very often.

1095
01:08:14.250 --> 01:08:15.918
That meant that
we could get a routine.

1096
01:08:16.000 --> 01:08:18.876
So we worked out.
we did trivia.

1097
01:08:18.959 --> 01:08:21.834
we had lectures.
and stuff like this.

1098
01:08:21.918 --> 01:08:24.667
We managed after.
I don't know. three weeks.

1099
01:08:24.751 --> 01:08:27.709
one month or so.
to make this Risk game.

1100
01:08:49.417 --> 01:08:51.209
We had a small bucket

1101
01:08:51.292 --> 01:08:52.792
that we received
some yogurt in.

1102
01:08:52.876 --> 01:08:56.334
and we cut out
a piece of cardboard

1103
01:08:56.417 --> 01:08:57.918
and we made three lines.

1104
01:08:58.000 --> 01:08:59.918
and we put it in
the bottom of the bucket

1105
01:09:00.000 --> 01:09:02.918
and then you should
hold up a date seed

1106
01:09:03.000 --> 01:09:04.751
and let it go.

1107
01:09:04.834 --> 01:09:06.209
It would fall down.

1108
01:09:06.292 --> 01:09:07.792
and whatever
number it landed on.

1109
01:09:07.876 --> 01:09:09.125
it would be that number.

1110
01:09:09.209 --> 01:09:11.459
So that was our dice
for the game.

1111
01:09:23.459 --> 01:09:27.125
Take 10 journalists. war.

1112
01:09:27.209 --> 01:09:29.584
put them into one room
and make them play

1113
01:09:29.667 --> 01:09:34.334
the game about taking
control over the world.

1114
01:09:34.417 --> 01:09:37.000
It's basically like putting
gasoline to a bonfire.

1115
01:10:11.417 --> 01:10:15.918
We started to have
our own small world.

1116
01:10:16.000 --> 01:10:19.834
That made everything much
easier to survive in a way.

1117
01:10:19.918 --> 01:10:21.918
It was much easier
to understand.

1118
01:10:24.083 --> 01:10:26.209
We didn't have to think
about economy.

1119
01:10:26.292 --> 01:10:29.626
We didn't have to think
about bank loans

1120
01:10:29.709 --> 01:10:34.459
or the prices of gas
at the moment.

1121
01:10:34.542 --> 01:10:37.209
You adapt
into the situation.

1122
01:10:37.292 --> 01:10:40.000
and then suddenly
this whole thing

1123
01:10:40.083 --> 01:10:42.500
becomes a part
of your life.

1124
01:10:42.584 --> 01:10:44.083
This is your life.

1125
01:10:44.167 --> 01:10:46.459
When you look back on it.
that's what I remember.

1126
01:10:46.542 --> 01:10:48.959
was our small society

1127
01:10:49.042 --> 01:10:51.626
where we really start
to know each other.

1128
01:10:51.709 --> 01:10:53.751
You know
who made this fart.

1129
01:10:53.834 --> 01:10:56.584
You could smell...
"This is the fart of you."

1130
01:11:18.584 --> 01:11:22.167
- I remember James'
- 40-year-old birthday.

1131
01:11:22.250 --> 01:11:25.083
It was late at night.
It was completely dark.

1132
01:11:25.167 --> 01:11:28.918
James just...
"Oh. by the way.

1133
01:11:29.000 --> 01:11:31.292
I turn 40 today."

1134
01:11:31.375 --> 01:11:32.792
I was just like. "What?"

1135
01:11:32.876 --> 01:11:34.626
So we sang
a song for him

1136
01:11:34.709 --> 01:11:37.042
and I remember
that we said

1137
01:11:37.125 --> 01:11:40.209
we hoped it would be a
much better birthday next year.

1138
01:12:19.500 --> 01:12:22.250
Right here.
One. two. and three.

1139
01:12:22.334 --> 01:12:24.083
Come on!
Smile! Please!

1140
01:12:24.167 --> 01:12:25.709
Nine! Good!

1141
01:12:25.792 --> 01:12:27.542
We've been through
a lot together.

1142
01:12:27.626 --> 01:12:30.709
Michael has cosigned
loans for me.

1143
01:12:32.042 --> 01:12:34.834
He has lent me
his professional clothes.

1144
01:12:34.918 --> 01:12:37.751
his car.
his dental plan.

1145
01:12:39.584 --> 01:12:42.292
I think sometimes we struggle
to understand each other

1146
01:12:42.375 --> 01:12:43.876
and where exactly
we're coming from

1147
01:12:43.959 --> 01:12:46.500
and why we do
the things we do.

1148
01:12:46.584 --> 01:12:50.375
Michael has entirely
too much common sense

1149
01:12:50.459 --> 01:12:54.250
and sometimes I have entirely
too little common sense.

1150
01:12:54.334 --> 01:12:58.626
We've somehow grown closer
despite the differences.

1151
01:12:58.709 --> 01:13:02.500
and it's... I guess
it's about being brothers.

1152
01:13:04.876 --> 01:13:08.709
- I'd say the first 100.
- 150 days I was all in.

1153
01:13:08.792 --> 01:13:13.500
but I definitely retracted
pretty strongly after that.

1154
01:13:13.584 --> 01:13:17.792
I have a lot of regrets about
not continuing full steam.

1155
01:13:17.876 --> 01:13:20.250
and I don't know
if it was because

1156
01:13:20.334 --> 01:13:22.584
I was trying
to protect myself

1157
01:13:22.667 --> 01:13:25.125
or I was just trying
to protect my family

1158
01:13:25.209 --> 01:13:28.876
and give the kids
the attention I have.

1159
01:13:28.959 --> 01:13:31.792
I could argue that that's
what Jim would prefer.

1160
01:13:31.876 --> 01:13:36.083
and I don't know... it was all
kinds of excuses or reasons.

1161
01:13:36.167 --> 01:13:39.500
but it's something
I do feel terrible about.

1162
01:13:39.584 --> 01:13:41.751
But then I got pulled
right back into it

1163
01:13:41.834 --> 01:13:43.542
very strongly
and very immediately

1164
01:13:43.626 --> 01:13:46.751
when the first e-mail
came to me.

1165
01:13:46.834 --> 01:13:49.042
"Hello. we have James

1166
01:13:49.125 --> 01:13:51.209
and want to
negotiate for him.

1167
01:13:51.292 --> 01:13:52.834
He is safe.

1168
01:13:52.918 --> 01:13:56.083
He is our friend and we
do not want to hurt him.

1169
01:13:56.167 --> 01:13:58.167
If you want cooperation.
we have rules.

1170
01:13:58.250 --> 01:14:01.250
You cannot go to the
media ever about this.

1171
01:14:01.334 --> 01:14:03.167
If you do.
we will not negotiate.

1172
01:14:03.250 --> 01:14:05.000
We want money fast."

1173
01:14:05.083 --> 01:14:07.542
We shared everything
we had with everybody.

1174
01:14:07.626 --> 01:14:10.292
FBI knew. security team
knew... everyone knew.

1175
01:14:10.375 --> 01:14:12.375
They said. "Just keep
them talking." and all that.

1176
01:14:12.459 --> 01:14:14.417
"They're just beginning
their negotiations.

1177
01:14:14.500 --> 01:14:16.667
We've got time.
Just keep at it."

1178
01:14:16.751 --> 01:14:19.292
So. I. after coordinating
with some officials.

1179
01:14:19.375 --> 01:14:21.959
replied to them. "We've
been concerned about Jim

1180
01:14:22.042 --> 01:14:23.751
and want to know
that he's okay.

1181
01:14:23.834 --> 01:14:26.542
Please provide us with proof
that you have Jim.

1182
01:14:26.626 --> 01:14:29.042
and we will be happy
to work things out with you."

1183
01:14:29.125 --> 01:14:32.042
We still didn't know
who was holding him.

1184
01:14:32.125 --> 01:14:35.292
It was obvious that they were
people against the Assad regime.

1185
01:14:35.375 --> 01:14:37.125
but they didn't
identify themselves

1186
01:14:37.209 --> 01:14:38.584
any more
than that at all.

1187
01:14:38.667 --> 01:14:40.709
They were very shrewd.

1188
01:14:40.792 --> 01:14:43.209
And their e-mails.
unfortunately.

1189
01:14:43.292 --> 01:14:45.042
were totally
undetectable.

1190
01:14:45.125 --> 01:14:47.876
About a week goes by
and they responded.

1191
01:14:47.959 --> 01:14:50.834
"James Wright Foley
is being detained by us.

1192
01:14:50.918 --> 01:14:52.792
At this stage. no video
or picture evidence

1193
01:14:52.876 --> 01:14:54.667
of his well-being
will be provided

1194
01:14:54.751 --> 01:14:57.751
until we see tangible progress
in your efforts to negotiate.

1195
01:14:57.834 --> 01:15:00.125
However. you will be allowed
to ask three questions

1196
01:15:00.209 --> 01:15:01.792
of a personal nature

1197
01:15:01.876 --> 01:15:04.250
that nobody except James
will know the answers to.

1198
01:15:04.334 --> 01:15:07.125
And our primary demand is that
you use your influence

1199
01:15:07.209 --> 01:15:08.500
to pressure
your government

1200
01:15:08.584 --> 01:15:10.459
to release
our Muslim prisoners

1201
01:15:10.542 --> 01:15:13.083
who they have imprisoned.
whether innocent

1202
01:15:13.167 --> 01:15:16.667
or 'guilty' according
to 'your laws.'

1203
01:15:16.751 --> 01:15:19.167
If this fails
to bear any fruits.

1204
01:15:19.250 --> 01:15:20.626
then our secondary demand

1205
01:15:20.709 --> 01:15:24.209
is the sum
of a hundred million euros."

1206
01:15:24.292 --> 01:15:27.876
FBI seemed to have
their hands tied

1207
01:15:27.959 --> 01:15:29.751
because all
they were able to do

1208
01:15:29.834 --> 01:15:33.209
was okay
our family e-mails.

1209
01:15:33.292 --> 01:15:37.709
and they really were unable
to help us much with strategy.

1210
01:15:37.792 --> 01:15:40.751
They just told us to be
yourselves. be family.

1211
01:15:40.834 --> 01:15:42.292
Tell them the truth

1212
01:15:42.375 --> 01:15:44.876
is we can't release
any prisoners.

1213
01:15:44.959 --> 01:15:47.751
We certainly don't have
a hundred million euro.

1214
01:15:47.834 --> 01:15:49.792
Michael came up
with some questions

1215
01:15:49.876 --> 01:15:51.667
I didn't know
the answers to.

1216
01:15:51.751 --> 01:15:56.334
but we sent those back
to the captors.

1217
01:15:56.417 --> 01:15:59.167
Then there was some
brief comment.

1218
01:15:59.250 --> 01:16:01.334
It says.
"James was detained

1219
01:16:01.417 --> 01:16:04.167
whilst operating
as a 'journalist.'

1220
01:16:04.250 --> 01:16:07.959
After his capture
and following interrogations.

1221
01:16:08.042 --> 01:16:09.751
we came to learn
that he had been embedded

1222
01:16:09.834 --> 01:16:11.751
with US troops
serving in Afghanistan

1223
01:16:11.834 --> 01:16:14.083
and that his brother
Michael Patrick Foley

1224
01:16:14.167 --> 01:16:16.751
is a serving officer
in the US Air Force."

1225
01:16:16.834 --> 01:16:18.417
But I was never
in the Air Force.

1226
01:16:18.500 --> 01:16:20.959
That was John.

1227
01:16:24.000 --> 01:16:27.751
My brother Jim
said that Mike

1228
01:16:27.834 --> 01:16:30.959
was the Air Force
officer.

1229
01:16:31.042 --> 01:16:33.167
It may have been a typo.
it may not have been.

1230
01:16:33.250 --> 01:16:37.459
But I believe
it was my big brother

1231
01:16:37.542 --> 01:16:40.209
trying to protect me.

1232
01:16:40.292 --> 01:16:44.709
You love
your brothers. but...

1233
01:16:49.417 --> 01:16:51.125
But for him to put...

1234
01:16:51.209 --> 01:16:53.209
Be willing to put
his life on the line

1235
01:16:53.292 --> 01:16:55.250
or his body
on the line...

1236
01:17:00.500 --> 01:17:03.042
for my protection
is significant.

1237
01:17:29.334 --> 01:17:32.500
And. I mean. it's...

1238
01:17:40.834 --> 01:17:43.918
I think they came in with
the proof of life for John

1239
01:17:44.000 --> 01:17:46.459
and then everybody else
got their proof of lives.

1240
01:17:46.542 --> 01:17:47.876
Everybody came in
like. "Yes!"

1241
01:17:47.959 --> 01:17:49.626
And we're happy and...

1242
01:17:49.709 --> 01:17:53.459
But James didn't get
his proof of life.

1243
01:17:53.542 --> 01:17:56.209
Until one day
that they came in

1244
01:17:56.292 --> 01:17:58.876
and they asked
James to follow.

1245
01:18:00.584 --> 01:18:02.083
And when he came back.
you know.

1246
01:18:02.167 --> 01:18:04.459
he came with his arms
over his head.

1247
01:18:04.542 --> 01:18:07.584
he said that this was
the best day of his life.

1248
01:18:07.667 --> 01:18:11.250
And then him and John.
they hugged each other

1249
01:18:11.334 --> 01:18:12.751
and they were
dancing around

1250
01:18:12.834 --> 01:18:16.042
like they just won
the big lottery.

1251
01:18:17.751 --> 01:18:20.626
- That was early.
- December of 2013.

1252
01:18:20.709 --> 01:18:22.667
All the answers
came back right on.

1253
01:18:22.751 --> 01:18:24.500
We knew they had Jim.

1254
01:18:24.584 --> 01:18:26.042
But by the end of December

1255
01:18:26.125 --> 01:18:28.209
they e-mailed us
back and said.

1256
01:18:28.292 --> 01:18:31.709
"This is the last e-mail
you'll get from us"

1257
01:18:31.792 --> 01:18:33.000
and cut off
communication.

1258
01:18:35.125 --> 01:18:37.000
John. Sr.: The first
year. we just trusted

1259
01:18:37.083 --> 01:18:38.918
that the government
would have this in hand

1260
01:18:39.000 --> 01:18:42.584
and despite our lack
of information. et cetera.

1261
01:18:42.667 --> 01:18:44.852
they had been through this
before and they knew what to do.

1262
01:18:44.876 --> 01:18:47.626
and we were in good hands.

1263
01:18:47.709 --> 01:18:51.167
At the end of that year. we
realized that nothing was being done

1264
01:18:51.250 --> 01:18:53.292
and that we were really

1265
01:18:53.375 --> 01:18:55.792
going to have to do
something on our own.

1266
01:19:05.167 --> 01:19:07.292
Diane was great
about meeting

1267
01:19:07.375 --> 01:19:10.000
with ambassadors
in Washington

1268
01:19:10.083 --> 01:19:11.751
from other countries

1269
01:19:11.834 --> 01:19:15.500
that might have
some influence.

1270
01:19:15.584 --> 01:19:18.834
It turned out that
no one had any influence.

1271
01:19:18.918 --> 01:19:21.000
because the group
that had him

1272
01:19:21.083 --> 01:19:23.584
didn't listen to anybody.
but we didn't know that.

1273
01:19:23.667 --> 01:19:25.918
It's kind of come as
a surprise to a lot of us.

1274
01:19:26.000 --> 01:19:28.476
this group ISIS. a group that we
hadn't really heard much about.

1275
01:19:28.500 --> 01:19:30.167
Who exactly are they?

1276
01:19:30.250 --> 01:19:32.334
Well. It's a criminal
marauding gang.

1277
01:19:32.417 --> 01:19:34.167
They come out
of the original.

1278
01:19:34.250 --> 01:19:38.042
very brutal
Iraqi terror group.

1279
01:19:38.125 --> 01:19:39.500
They're the worst.

1280
01:19:39.584 --> 01:19:41.459
They're the worst
of the worst.

1281
01:19:41.542 --> 01:19:43.751
Washington doesn't know
how to deal with them.

1282
01:19:43.834 --> 01:19:45.083
How's this family
in New England

1283
01:19:45.167 --> 01:19:46.792
going to figure
that out. right?

1284
01:19:46.876 --> 01:19:50.626
I mean you're dealing
with pure evil.

1285
01:19:50.709 --> 01:19:55.209
but a capable
and organized group.

1286
01:21:09.042 --> 01:21:11.375
They were very tough
towards me. also.

1287
01:21:11.459 --> 01:21:15.167
But no matter what.
they meant freedom for me.

1288
01:21:15.250 --> 01:21:18.626
because they were the ones
negotiating for me.

1289
01:21:18.709 --> 01:21:21.459
But James and John
were destroyed

1290
01:21:21.542 --> 01:21:23.792
by the Beatles
in the beginning.

1291
01:21:31.542 --> 01:21:33.959
He had this ability

1292
01:21:34.042 --> 01:21:36.083
to escape the situation.

1293
01:21:36.167 --> 01:21:40.626
To enjoy. you know.
the sound of children

1294
01:21:40.709 --> 01:21:42.292
playing outside.

1295
01:21:45.500 --> 01:21:49.959
Or enjoyed the view
of just some sun

1296
01:21:50.042 --> 01:21:52.292
entering through
the window.

1297
01:22:26.792 --> 01:22:28.792
James converted
in the beginning

1298
01:22:28.876 --> 01:22:30.584
of his captivity.

1299
01:22:33.042 --> 01:22:36.125
And I know that
was at the same period

1300
01:22:36.209 --> 01:22:39.334
as he were getting
really bad treatment.

1301
01:22:41.250 --> 01:22:43.792
It gave a good routine.

1302
01:22:43.876 --> 01:22:47.042
Normally. we have a tradition of
going into church every Sunday.

1303
01:22:47.125 --> 01:22:48.792
But if you cannot do that.

1304
01:22:48.876 --> 01:22:50.959
you need another way
of feeling

1305
01:22:51.042 --> 01:22:53.876
that you are doing
something with your faith.

1306
01:22:55.459 --> 01:22:57.709
You can call it
a surviving skill.

1307
01:22:57.792 --> 01:22:59.584
You can call it
just a way

1308
01:22:59.667 --> 01:23:03.375
of being interested
in another culture.

1309
01:23:03.459 --> 01:23:05.042
What James used
to say to me

1310
01:23:05.125 --> 01:23:08.292
was that for him
God is the same.

1311
01:23:38.792 --> 01:23:40.083
We had such
great Christmases

1312
01:23:40.167 --> 01:23:41.626
when the kids were little.

1313
01:23:41.709 --> 01:23:44.876
Just wonderful.
wonderful Christmases.

1314
01:23:44.959 --> 01:23:46.834
And. you know.
as they got older

1315
01:23:46.918 --> 01:23:49.209
and Jim's
siblings married.

1316
01:23:49.292 --> 01:23:51.292
they weren't always
able to be home.

1317
01:23:51.375 --> 01:23:53.542
But Jim was always home.

1318
01:23:53.626 --> 01:23:57.751
Most of my memories of Jim
culminate around the holidays.

1319
01:23:57.834 --> 01:23:59.542
You know. the playing
of the video games.

1320
01:23:59.626 --> 01:24:02.626
the board games.
the Ping-Pong tournaments.

1321
01:24:02.709 --> 01:24:05.584
the anything tournaments.

1322
01:24:08.542 --> 01:24:10.876
John. Jr.: And you wake
up and you open the gifts.

1323
01:24:10.959 --> 01:24:13.959
and you're like.
"Crap. Jim has my name.

1324
01:24:14.042 --> 01:24:16.167
What's he going to
give me?"

1325
01:24:18.792 --> 01:24:20.876
I shouldn't say that.

1326
01:24:22.042 --> 01:24:24.292
It was good times.

1327
01:24:24.375 --> 01:24:27.083
And then the last
Christmas he was home.

1328
01:24:27.167 --> 01:24:30.083
I remember him
rolling on the floor

1329
01:24:30.167 --> 01:24:32.334
with Michael's son Matty.

1330
01:24:32.417 --> 01:24:34.083
He loved that kid.

1331
01:24:35.959 --> 01:24:38.167
There's a million
Christmas stories. though.

1332
01:24:44.125 --> 01:24:45.584
It's difficult for us

1333
01:24:45.667 --> 01:24:48.000
to celebrate Christmas
in any way.

1334
01:24:48.083 --> 01:24:50.876
We didn't have any presents
to give each other.

1335
01:24:50.959 --> 01:24:56.000
So. we decided
to sit down in a circle.

1336
01:24:56.083 --> 01:25:00.459
and then we had to say
something nice to each other.

1337
01:25:00.542 --> 01:25:03.709
And I remember that
I said to James that.

1338
01:25:03.792 --> 01:25:05.500
"James. the first time
I met you was.

1339
01:25:05.584 --> 01:25:07.167
you know.
in this prison.

1340
01:25:07.250 --> 01:25:10.083
and you looked
as confused

1341
01:25:10.167 --> 01:25:12.876
as if you were just
dumped down on the Earth

1342
01:25:12.959 --> 01:25:14.250
from the moon
or something.

1343
01:25:14.334 --> 01:25:17.792
And you basically
destroyed my whole idea

1344
01:25:17.876 --> 01:25:22.375
of this great war journalist
James Wright Foley.

1345
01:25:22.459 --> 01:25:26.709
And then suddenly I find out
that you are very clumsy.

1346
01:25:26.792 --> 01:25:28.709
you're very bad at sports.

1347
01:25:30.459 --> 01:25:34.042
But then again. you...

1348
01:25:34.125 --> 01:25:36.042
You're the most..."

1349
01:25:36.125 --> 01:25:38.375
I think I said to him.

1350
01:25:38.459 --> 01:25:40.500
"You're the most
honest person.

1351
01:25:40.584 --> 01:25:45.334
There is no evil at all
to find in you. James.

1352
01:25:45.417 --> 01:25:48.834
You are pure good.
Sometimes too good.

1353
01:25:48.918 --> 01:25:52.584
And I'm really happy
that I've met you."

1354
01:25:57.042 --> 01:25:59.042
That was
our Christmas night.

1355
01:25:59.125 --> 01:26:01.500
When I went to bed that night.
I really felt that I had

1356
01:26:01.584 --> 01:26:04.209
the best Christmas night
in my life.

1357
01:27:55.459 --> 01:27:57.626
So that's when I started
to get more frantic.

1358
01:27:57.709 --> 01:27:59.792
I mean. I thought there's
got to be some way

1359
01:27:59.876 --> 01:28:03.209
to get the French
and the US to talk.

1360
01:28:03.292 --> 01:28:05.083
Didier.

1361
01:28:05.167 --> 01:28:07.876
This is
my husband John.

1362
01:28:07.959 --> 01:28:10.959
They were willing to share all
kinds of things about Jim...

1363
01:28:11.042 --> 01:28:13.626
Personally. how he was.
what they did.

1364
01:28:13.709 --> 01:28:15.500
So I was starting
to get all this hope.

1365
01:28:15.584 --> 01:28:19.042
And they have a
hostage crisis unit in Paris

1366
01:28:19.125 --> 01:28:21.584
and they were very generous
with their time.

1367
01:28:21.667 --> 01:28:23.918
So it was so different than
what I was experiencing.

1368
01:28:24.000 --> 01:28:26.542
I was just kind of like.
"Jeez." you know?

1369
01:28:26.626 --> 01:28:28.125
Jim and the others...

1370
01:28:28.209 --> 01:28:29.709
Our other Americans
are as important

1371
01:28:29.792 --> 01:28:31.209
as these guys.
aren't they?

1372
01:28:32.959 --> 01:28:37.125
And it was the last
night I was in Paris

1373
01:28:37.209 --> 01:28:39.167
and I got a phone call
from John.

1374
01:28:39.250 --> 01:28:44.000
He said. "Diane. we got
another e-mail from the captors."

1375
01:28:44.083 --> 01:28:47.083
I thought. "Oh. great.
What did they say?"

1376
01:28:47.167 --> 01:28:50.709
And that's when they
threatened to kill Jim.

1377
01:28:55.167 --> 01:28:59.626
But me.
in my cluelessness.

1378
01:28:59.709 --> 01:29:03.417
I was just excited that
they had reached out to us.

1379
01:29:03.500 --> 01:29:07.375
We had raised about
$1 million in pledges

1380
01:29:07.459 --> 01:29:09.417
and so I was so excited
to hear from them.

1381
01:29:09.500 --> 01:29:12.042
because we thought. "Now we
can tell them we have this money."

1382
01:29:12.125 --> 01:29:14.626
I was just so clueless.

1383
01:29:16.542 --> 01:29:19.751
We knew that paying
a ransom was illegal.

1384
01:29:19.834 --> 01:29:24.375
and we also knew that it
wouldn't have stopped us.

1385
01:29:24.459 --> 01:29:28.042
The Foleys were prepared
to mortgage their house

1386
01:29:28.125 --> 01:29:32.542
and do what needed to be done
to make a payment.

1387
01:29:32.626 --> 01:29:35.375
I have a lot
of evolving thoughts

1388
01:29:35.459 --> 01:29:37.042
about this whole process

1389
01:29:37.125 --> 01:29:40.209
and what
the government didn't do

1390
01:29:40.292 --> 01:29:42.459
that it should have done.

1391
01:29:42.542 --> 01:29:46.417
I mean. if you just
look at the facts.

1392
01:29:46.500 --> 01:29:49.000
there are 15 European
hostages

1393
01:29:49.083 --> 01:29:53.042
who are alive and with
their families and friends

1394
01:29:53.125 --> 01:29:54.834
and loved ones today.

1395
01:29:57.876 --> 01:30:00.751
I wish we had started
raising money sooner.

1396
01:30:02.542 --> 01:30:04.375
I wish we had negotiated.

1397
01:30:04.459 --> 01:30:07.042
I wish it had
turned out differently.

1398
01:31:02.292 --> 01:31:05.626
In some sense. I was okay
with the fact that he got captured.

1399
01:31:05.709 --> 01:31:09.375
because I knew he was
doing what he wanted to do.

1400
01:31:09.459 --> 01:31:13.334
And as a good family member
and as a good brother.

1401
01:31:13.417 --> 01:31:15.083
I need to understand that.

1402
01:31:15.167 --> 01:31:19.292
My mom did a great job
in keeping faith

1403
01:31:19.375 --> 01:31:21.459
and working as hard
as she could.

1404
01:31:21.542 --> 01:31:23.959
but from the get-go
once it happened.

1405
01:31:24.042 --> 01:31:26.334
I kind of felt like
he was already gone.

1406
01:31:32.083 --> 01:31:34.667
So after a group
of the other hostages

1407
01:31:34.751 --> 01:31:36.667
was released.
the Beatles.

1408
01:31:36.751 --> 01:31:39.459
they came back
the day after.

1409
01:31:39.542 --> 01:31:42.792
and they...

1410
01:31:42.876 --> 01:31:47.083
They beat the shit
out of James and I.

1411
01:31:48.709 --> 01:31:50.667
I think it's called
a charley horse.

1412
01:31:50.751 --> 01:31:54.918
when you put your knee
into the legs of a person.

1413
01:31:55.000 --> 01:31:58.334
They did that
to me and James

1414
01:31:58.417 --> 01:32:01.751
while we had to sit
in stress positions.

1415
01:32:01.834 --> 01:32:03.417
I don't know
how many they gave us.

1416
01:32:03.500 --> 01:32:06.250
but it just continued.

1417
01:32:06.334 --> 01:32:09.959
I never tried anything that
hurt so much in my entire life.

1418
01:32:12.626 --> 01:32:15.167
And then they just left.

1419
01:32:17.125 --> 01:32:18.792
I was just laying
there crying.

1420
01:32:18.876 --> 01:32:22.667
and I couldn't be
in my own body of pure pain.

1421
01:32:22.751 --> 01:32:26.667
And James in the other
corner of the room.

1422
01:32:26.751 --> 01:32:29.792
I knew he got exactly
the same treatment as me.

1423
01:32:29.876 --> 01:32:32.083
but I couldn't hear it.

1424
01:32:32.167 --> 01:32:34.250
He just...

1425
01:32:34.334 --> 01:32:37.334
I don't know if he kept
it inside or how he did it.

1426
01:32:37.417 --> 01:32:40.500
but only a few minutes
after they left James.

1427
01:32:40.584 --> 01:32:43.792
he kind of looked up
and asked me if I was okay.

1428
01:32:43.876 --> 01:32:46.125
I was like.
"Shut up. James."

1429
01:32:46.209 --> 01:32:48.751
I remember. I said.
"Shut up. James."

1430
01:32:51.000 --> 01:32:53.709
"Don't ask me
if I'm okay."

1431
01:32:53.792 --> 01:32:56.751
"Don't worry about me.
Worry about yourself."

1432
01:32:58.876 --> 01:33:00.751
Yeah. we just laid there

1433
01:33:00.834 --> 01:33:04.000
until the pain started
to disappear a bit.

1434
01:33:05.918 --> 01:33:09.125
Two or three days after.
they came in again.

1435
01:33:09.209 --> 01:33:12.000
This time. the Beatles
just told us that.

1436
01:33:12.083 --> 01:33:14.167
"Now. guys.
everything's changed."

1437
01:33:14.250 --> 01:33:18.292
And they took all
our food. our games.

1438
01:33:18.375 --> 01:33:19.918
most of the blankets.

1439
01:33:22.250 --> 01:33:25.667
They started to use
police clubs.

1440
01:33:25.751 --> 01:33:27.191
So. every time
we went to the toilet.

1441
01:33:27.250 --> 01:33:29.918
we got beatings
with a stick.

1442
01:33:30.000 --> 01:33:34.292
We were so scared.
We lost all hope.

1443
01:33:34.375 --> 01:33:38.334
No matter what came
through that door. it was evil.

1444
01:33:38.417 --> 01:33:41.209
Those 14 days
before I got released

1445
01:33:41.292 --> 01:33:44.042
was the absolute
worst times.

1446
01:33:50.000 --> 01:33:52.209
When people started
to get released.

1447
01:33:52.292 --> 01:33:55.042
we decided
to send out letters

1448
01:33:55.125 --> 01:33:58.542
with the person
who was released.

1449
01:33:58.626 --> 01:34:00.209
But James. he didn't.

1450
01:34:00.292 --> 01:34:02.459
He didn't want
to bother any of us.

1451
01:34:02.542 --> 01:34:07.209
And I remember one day that
I saw that John and James

1452
01:34:07.292 --> 01:34:10.959
had been talking
for some time in the corner.

1453
01:34:11.042 --> 01:34:13.167
And after that. John.
he came over to me and said.

1454
01:34:13.250 --> 01:34:15.417
"Daniel. James. he wants
to ask you something."

1455
01:34:15.500 --> 01:34:17.626
"Okay. Okay.
What's up. James?"

1456
01:34:17.709 --> 01:34:20.542
And he's like.
"Oh. uh. it's just if...

1457
01:34:20.626 --> 01:34:23.000
If you want to carry out
a message or something."

1458
01:34:23.083 --> 01:34:25.459
He was very...
He said it fast.

1459
01:34:25.542 --> 01:34:28.125
like he didn't
want to bother me.

1460
01:34:30.125 --> 01:34:33.125
There's many ways of
dealing with a situation like this.

1461
01:34:33.209 --> 01:34:37.751
And James. I think one of the
reasons why he remained so strong

1462
01:34:37.834 --> 01:34:41.918
was because he managed to
think about all the good things.

1463
01:34:42.000 --> 01:34:46.000
He saw the light
instead of the dark spots.

1464
01:34:46.083 --> 01:34:50.334
where a guy like John.
he was much more realistic.

1465
01:34:50.417 --> 01:34:52.792
He knew when it was bad.

1466
01:34:56.918 --> 01:34:58.834
But I couldn't
bring out the letter.

1467
01:34:58.918 --> 01:35:01.918
I was too afraid
after these 14 days.

1468
01:35:02.000 --> 01:35:05.709
so I decided just to
memorize it as fast as I could.

1469
01:35:05.792 --> 01:35:07.792
So I started waiting.

1470
01:35:07.876 --> 01:35:10.083
I knew they will come in
the morning to pick me up.

1471
01:35:10.167 --> 01:35:12.751
One day went.

1472
01:35:12.834 --> 01:35:15.083
Two days went.

1473
01:35:15.167 --> 01:35:18.834
And then fourth day.
fifth day.

1474
01:35:18.918 --> 01:35:20.626
Shit. man.
Then I woke up there

1475
01:35:20.709 --> 01:35:23.959
the sixth day in the morning.
and I couldn't sleep.

1476
01:35:24.042 --> 01:35:26.042
I couldn't sleep all night
because I was so afraid

1477
01:35:26.125 --> 01:35:28.626
of what if they are
playing a trick on me.

1478
01:35:30.417 --> 01:35:32.125
And James.
he walked over to me.

1479
01:35:32.209 --> 01:35:34.292
and he sat down
right next to me

1480
01:35:34.375 --> 01:35:36.250
and he said.
"Are you okay. Daniel?"

1481
01:35:36.334 --> 01:35:38.709
I said. "Yeah."

1482
01:35:38.792 --> 01:35:40.417
Then I just couldn't
hold it back.

1483
01:35:40.500 --> 01:35:44.667
and I said. "Fuck. man.
I am really. really scared.

1484
01:35:44.751 --> 01:35:46.918
I'm really. really. really.
really scared right now.

1485
01:35:47.000 --> 01:35:49.083
I don't know what to do.
what to think. I...

1486
01:35:49.167 --> 01:35:51.584
And he said.
"Daniel. calm down.

1487
01:35:51.667 --> 01:35:53.125
Calm down.

1488
01:35:53.209 --> 01:35:54.959
Everything will be fine.

1489
01:35:55.042 --> 01:35:56.626
You are going home.

1490
01:35:56.709 --> 01:36:00.000
They will come in
a few hours or tomorrow.

1491
01:36:00.083 --> 01:36:01.667
but one thing
is sure. Daniel.

1492
01:36:01.751 --> 01:36:03.834
You will go home."

1493
01:36:03.918 --> 01:36:05.667
Once again.
it was a real feeling.

1494
01:36:05.751 --> 01:36:07.375
because I was
sitting there crying

1495
01:36:07.459 --> 01:36:11.209
and making a scene
in front of James.

1496
01:36:11.292 --> 01:36:13.083
and I was about to go home

1497
01:36:13.167 --> 01:36:15.959
and. James. he didn't have
anything to look forward to.

1498
01:36:17.500 --> 01:36:20.417
James. he went back to
his side of the room.

1499
01:36:24.584 --> 01:36:28.042
10 minutes after.
they knocked on the door.

1500
01:36:28.125 --> 01:36:30.626
they came in and asked me
to put a blanket over my head

1501
01:36:30.709 --> 01:36:32.751
and follow them.

1502
01:36:36.417 --> 01:36:39.125
That was
the last time I...

1503
01:36:39.209 --> 01:36:40.709
I saw James.

1504
01:37:02.083 --> 01:37:04.042
I call on my friends.
family. and loved ones

1505
01:37:04.125 --> 01:37:08.042
to rise up against my real
killers. the US government.

1506
01:37:08.125 --> 01:37:09.918
For what will happen to me

1507
01:37:10.000 --> 01:37:13.459
is only a result of their
complacency and criminality.

1508
01:37:13.542 --> 01:37:15.792
I wish I had more time.

1509
01:37:15.876 --> 01:37:17.393
I wish I could have
the hope of freedom

1510
01:37:17.417 --> 01:37:19.417
and seeing my family
once again.

1511
01:37:19.500 --> 01:37:21.250
but that ship has sailed.

1512
01:37:23.209 --> 01:37:26.834
I hadn't heard Jim's voice
in two years.

1513
01:37:29.250 --> 01:37:33.042
I guess you can see it
in his neck. in his face...

1514
01:37:33.125 --> 01:37:36.792
Just this strength
he has at that moment.

1515
01:37:38.375 --> 01:37:41.250
And I think of myself.

1516
01:37:41.334 --> 01:37:44.209
I'd be calling for
my mother or something.

1517
01:37:44.292 --> 01:37:48.250
Just the strength
he had and I...

1518
01:37:48.334 --> 01:37:52.417
I wanted to feel
what he felt.

1519
01:37:52.500 --> 01:37:54.709
was the reason
I watched it.

1520
01:37:58.542 --> 01:38:01.584
I kept getting messages
saying. "John. are you okay?

1521
01:38:01.667 --> 01:38:04.667
John. are you okay about
what your brother said?"

1522
01:38:04.751 --> 01:38:07.042
I'm like. "What are you
talking about?"

1523
01:38:07.125 --> 01:38:08.918
I call on my brother John.

1524
01:38:09.000 --> 01:38:12.459
who is a member
of the US Air Force.

1525
01:38:12.542 --> 01:38:15.125
Think about
what you are doing.

1526
01:38:15.209 --> 01:38:17.709
Think about
the lives you destroy.

1527
01:38:17.792 --> 01:38:19.792
including those
of your own family.

1528
01:38:22.375 --> 01:38:24.250
Even though he was
reading the script.

1529
01:38:24.334 --> 01:38:28.751
he seemed defiant
to the end.

1530
01:38:30.417 --> 01:38:31.626
I mean. for sure.

1531
01:38:31.709 --> 01:38:36.417
I still have some guilt
regarding me and...

1532
01:38:36.500 --> 01:38:40.626
Just me and.
unfortunately. my profession.

1533
01:38:40.709 --> 01:38:44.417
but I'm sure that
he endured torture.

1534
01:38:45.876 --> 01:38:47.500
Interestingly.
John and I have...

1535
01:38:47.584 --> 01:38:51.709
We've argued a lot
about things since that.

1536
01:38:51.792 --> 01:38:54.334
but we've been
communicating a lot more.

1537
01:38:54.417 --> 01:38:57.250
We've come a lot closer
as a result of it.

1538
01:38:57.334 --> 01:39:00.292
I think Jim
would have loved that.

1539
01:39:00.375 --> 01:39:02.334
You know Jim
as well as I do.

1540
01:39:02.417 --> 01:39:04.727
He wouldn't have said those
things if there wasn't someone else

1541
01:39:04.751 --> 01:39:06.751
that was going to be harmed
as a result of it.

1542
01:39:06.834 --> 01:39:08.626
He never cared
about himself.

1543
01:39:10.918 --> 01:39:13.083
My only thought
when I found out about Jim

1544
01:39:13.167 --> 01:39:14.959
was how am I
going to get home?

1545
01:39:15.042 --> 01:39:17.542
I had missed
the initial chaos

1546
01:39:17.626 --> 01:39:19.083
of when it all happened.

1547
01:39:19.167 --> 01:39:23.000
but slowly we all
kind of came together.

1548
01:39:23.083 --> 01:39:26.375
We literally chose the smallest
room possible in our house.

1549
01:39:26.459 --> 01:39:28.792
but everybody was just
kind of huddled together.

1550
01:39:30.792 --> 01:39:33.542
Jim always found a way
to bring people together.

1551
01:39:33.626 --> 01:39:35.667
Always.

1552
01:39:38.459 --> 01:39:40.542
I really don't think

1553
01:39:40.626 --> 01:39:43.083
I came to know
Jim as a man.

1554
01:39:43.167 --> 01:39:45.209
as an adult.

1555
01:39:45.292 --> 01:39:47.667
I came to know him
through his friends.

1556
01:39:50.918 --> 01:39:52.751
It's hard as a mother.

1557
01:39:52.834 --> 01:39:56.209
You know. they're always
your children somehow.

1558
01:39:58.083 --> 01:40:00.834
And it's hard to see
that they're adults

1559
01:40:00.918 --> 01:40:04.584
with their own gifts and
their own way in the world.

1560
01:40:07.250 --> 01:40:10.083
The day before
his memorial.

1561
01:40:10.167 --> 01:40:13.876
I was like. "Shit."

1562
01:40:13.959 --> 01:40:16.709
I was like.
"Now I get it.

1563
01:40:16.792 --> 01:40:19.125
Now I get it."

1564
01:40:19.209 --> 01:40:23.876
Jokingly. I was always like.
you know. "Jim. get a job.

1565
01:40:23.959 --> 01:40:27.626
Jim. save for retirement."

1566
01:40:27.709 --> 01:40:29.751
What I didn't recognize.

1567
01:40:29.834 --> 01:40:31.542
he was really trying
to teach me.

1568
01:40:31.626 --> 01:40:35.834
"John. you need to look
outside of yourself.

1569
01:40:35.918 --> 01:40:40.876
John. it's not about
physical or monetary things.

1570
01:40:40.959 --> 01:40:43.459
It's about how you act.

1571
01:40:43.542 --> 01:40:47.459
It's who you teach.
who you mentor.

1572
01:40:47.542 --> 01:40:49.959
Who's going
to remember you?

1573
01:40:50.042 --> 01:40:51.959
What are they going
to remember about you?"

1574
01:40:56.042 --> 01:40:57.876
John. Sr.:
Jimmy was included

1575
01:40:57.959 --> 01:40:59.459
in the long line
of journalists

1576
01:40:59.542 --> 01:41:02.125
who gave their lives
to tell the truth.

1577
01:41:02.209 --> 01:41:03.626
We were just totally humbled

1578
01:41:03.709 --> 01:41:06.417
by the fact that
the committee in Bayeux

1579
01:41:06.500 --> 01:41:09.876
would nominate him
to be included

1580
01:41:09.959 --> 01:41:11.500
on one of the markers

1581
01:41:11.584 --> 01:41:14.918
with 2.000 other deceased
journalists.

1582
01:41:15.000 --> 01:41:18.125
It was a very
important moment.

1583
01:41:20.584 --> 01:41:22.250
This was Jim.

1584
01:41:22.334 --> 01:41:26.250
and it made me
feel very warm inside

1585
01:41:26.334 --> 01:41:30.375
that he was accepted
as one of them.

1586
01:41:54.459 --> 01:41:56.185
John. Sr.: And that's
the difference with Jim.

1587
01:41:56.209 --> 01:41:58.459
He was just
a you and a me...

1588
01:41:58.542 --> 01:42:02.209
A friendly guy...
Until tested.

1589
01:42:04.167 --> 01:42:06.542
Where that came from...
I don't know. Brian.

1590
01:42:11.000 --> 01:42:14.709
Because you just can't grit
something like that out.

1591
01:42:14.792 --> 01:42:19.209
You have to have
an inner strength

1592
01:42:19.292 --> 01:42:20.292
to do that.

1593
01:42:22.417 --> 01:42:25.209
We discovered Jim
just like the world did.

1594
01:42:28.042 --> 01:42:30.042
He was truly
a man for others.

1595
01:42:34.375 --> 01:42:36.876
Witnessing Jim's murder
this publicly.

1596
01:42:36.959 --> 01:42:39.167
it sends a message
to all of us.

1597
01:42:39.250 --> 01:42:42.959
And my. I guess.
response to that

1598
01:42:43.042 --> 01:42:45.959
is having lost
so many friends

1599
01:42:46.042 --> 01:42:48.334
and knowing that people

1600
01:42:48.417 --> 01:42:51.375
are purposely out
for journalists now.

1601
01:42:51.459 --> 01:42:53.375
I mean.
we have to fight back

1602
01:42:53.459 --> 01:42:58.083
with our pictures
and our words.

1603
01:42:58.167 --> 01:43:00.250
I don't want to
let them win.

1604
01:43:02.250 --> 01:43:06.626
The extent to which the media
coverage took off.

1605
01:43:06.709 --> 01:43:09.626
it was just staggering. It
was absolutely staggering.

1606
01:43:09.709 --> 01:43:13.375
Something like
94% of Americans

1607
01:43:13.459 --> 01:43:16.876
were aware of Jim's death
and how he died.

1608
01:43:16.959 --> 01:43:20.375
It's the event with
the second most recognition

1609
01:43:20.459 --> 01:43:23.167
in recent American history
after 9/11.

1610
01:43:23.250 --> 01:43:25.083
He would have been
horrified by that.

1611
01:43:25.167 --> 01:43:28.000
I mean. he was there to talk
about the Syrian people

1612
01:43:28.083 --> 01:43:30.083
and this is the takeaway.

1613
01:43:33.125 --> 01:43:35.334
What is your response
to people

1614
01:43:35.417 --> 01:43:38.542
who would say he shouldn't
have been in there?

1615
01:43:38.626 --> 01:43:41.959
My response is do you
read the newspaper?

1616
01:43:42.042 --> 01:43:45.042
Do you watch TV?
You're depending on someone

1617
01:43:45.125 --> 01:43:47.542
to bring you
that information.

1618
01:43:47.626 --> 01:43:50.459
If you care about
what's going on in Syria.

1619
01:43:50.542 --> 01:43:52.667
you don't have
the right to be like.

1620
01:43:52.751 --> 01:43:53.959
"Oh. why's he there?"

1621
01:43:54.042 --> 01:43:55.542
How do you even
know what there is?

1622
01:43:55.626 --> 01:43:57.542
Because he told you.

1623
01:44:02.292 --> 01:44:03.626
I remember the video.

1624
01:44:03.709 --> 01:44:06.000
and there was a desert.

1625
01:44:07.751 --> 01:44:09.751
And in the background

1626
01:44:09.834 --> 01:44:12.375
you could see that
the desert stopped.

1627
01:44:12.459 --> 01:44:16.584
and there was all
the Valley of Euphrates.

1628
01:44:16.667 --> 01:44:20.959
So. according to what
I know from James.

1629
01:44:21.042 --> 01:44:23.918
I'm. like. 100% sure

1630
01:44:24.000 --> 01:44:27.918
that even though he was
convinced he would be killed.

1631
01:44:28.000 --> 01:44:29.751
he enjoyed the view.

1632
01:44:33.792 --> 01:44:36.250
He died as a free man.

1633
01:44:36.334 --> 01:44:38.876
This is not the death
of a hostage.

1634
01:44:38.959 --> 01:44:41.125
And this is the difference.
eventually.

1635
01:44:41.209 --> 01:44:44.667
between Jim and myself.

1636
01:44:44.751 --> 01:44:48.918
I ended up being released.

1637
01:44:49.000 --> 01:44:51.709
but he ended up free.

1638
01:44:57.292 --> 01:45:00.250
And when we came
to James' funeral.

1639
01:45:00.334 --> 01:45:03.876
everything started to become
real again in a way.

1640
01:45:03.959 --> 01:45:06.250
The fact that I managed
to say good-bye

1641
01:45:06.334 --> 01:45:08.584
to James together
with all his loved ones

1642
01:45:08.667 --> 01:45:10.042
was really powerful.

1643
01:45:10.125 --> 01:45:12.792
It reminded me
how important it was that...

1644
01:45:12.876 --> 01:45:16.626
That. James. he gave
that letter to me.

1645
01:45:24.918 --> 01:45:26.459
"Dear family and friends.

1646
01:45:26.542 --> 01:45:29.542
I remember going to
the mall with Dad.

1647
01:45:29.626 --> 01:45:32.083
a very long
bike ride with Mom.

1648
01:45:32.167 --> 01:45:34.083
I remember so many
great family times

1649
01:45:34.167 --> 01:45:36.667
that take me away
from this prison.

1650
01:45:36.751 --> 01:45:39.000
Dreams of family
and friends take me away

1651
01:45:39.083 --> 01:45:41.250
and happiness
fills my heart.

1652
01:45:43.375 --> 01:45:46.042
I know you're thinking
of me and praying for me.

1653
01:45:46.125 --> 01:45:48.375
and I am so thankful.

1654
01:45:48.459 --> 01:45:50.500
I feel you all.
especially when I pray.

1655
01:45:50.584 --> 01:45:53.584
I pray for you to stay
strong and to believe.

1656
01:45:53.667 --> 01:45:54.959
I really feel
I can touch you.

1657
01:45:55.042 --> 01:45:57.042
even in this darkness.
when I pray.

1658
01:46:02.709 --> 01:46:04.959
I think a lot about
my brothers and sister.

1659
01:46:05.042 --> 01:46:08.375
I remember playing werewolf
in the dark with Michael

1660
01:46:08.459 --> 01:46:10.125
and so many other
adventures.

1661
01:46:12.250 --> 01:46:15.876
I think of chasing Matty and
T. Around the kitchen counter.

1662
01:46:15.959 --> 01:46:18.042
It makes me happy
to think of them.

1663
01:46:18.125 --> 01:46:20.292
If there is any money
left in my bank account.

1664
01:46:20.375 --> 01:46:23.417
I want it to go
to Michael and Matthew.

1665
01:46:23.500 --> 01:46:25.959
I am so proud of you.
Michael.

1666
01:46:26.042 --> 01:46:28.667
and thankful to you
for happy childhood memories

1667
01:46:28.751 --> 01:46:31.083
and to you and Kristie
for happy adult ones.

1668
01:46:35.375 --> 01:46:36.918
And. Big John.

1669
01:46:37.000 --> 01:46:39.626
how I enjoyed visiting you
and Cress in Germany.

1670
01:46:39.709 --> 01:46:41.500
Thank you
for welcoming me.

1671
01:46:41.584 --> 01:46:42.959
I think a lot about RoRo

1672
01:46:43.042 --> 01:46:45.167
and try to imagine
what Jack is like.

1673
01:46:45.250 --> 01:46:48.167
I hope he has RoRo's
personality.

1674
01:46:48.250 --> 01:46:49.834
So. Mark and Kasey.

1675
01:46:49.918 --> 01:46:53.250
what name have you
given your son?

1676
01:46:53.334 --> 01:46:55.042
James Michael Foley.

1677
01:46:55.125 --> 01:46:57.500
James Michael Foley.

1678
01:46:57.584 --> 01:47:01.959
And. Mark. so proud
of you. too. bro.

1679
01:47:02.042 --> 01:47:03.792
I think of you
on the West Coast

1680
01:47:03.876 --> 01:47:05.894
and hope you're doing some
snowboarding and camping.

1681
01:47:05.918 --> 01:47:07.417
I especially remember us

1682
01:47:07.500 --> 01:47:09.459
going to the comedy club
in Boston together

1683
01:47:09.542 --> 01:47:12.459
and our big hug after.

1684
01:47:12.542 --> 01:47:14.542
The special moments
keep me hopeful.

1685
01:47:17.918 --> 01:47:20.459
Katie. so very
proud of you.

1686
01:47:20.542 --> 01:47:23.042
You are the strongest
and best of us all.

1687
01:47:23.125 --> 01:47:24.667
I think of you
working so hard

1688
01:47:24.751 --> 01:47:27.000
helping people
as a nurse.

1689
01:47:27.083 --> 01:47:29.626
I pray I can come
to your wedding.

1690
01:47:29.709 --> 01:47:31.500
Now I'm sounding
like Grammy.

1691
01:47:33.876 --> 01:47:35.792
Grammy. please
take your medicine.

1692
01:47:35.876 --> 01:47:38.500
Take walks
and keep dancing.

1693
01:47:38.584 --> 01:47:41.125
I plan to take you out to
Margaritas when I get home.

1694
01:47:41.209 --> 01:47:44.042
Stay strong. because I am
going to need your help

1695
01:47:44.125 --> 01:47:47.375
to reclaim my life.
Jim."

